PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L06U4.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":6,"cefr":"B1b","unit":4,"theme":"Food Around the World","essential_question":"What does food tell us about people and places?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["cuisine","recipe","ingredient","spice","flavor","dish","traditional","harvest","grain","bake","grill","portion","nutritious","crop"],"recycled_words":["food","rice","first","next","finally","compare","contrast","both","but","culture","tradition","recipe","ingredient"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS1","title":"Word Power: Food Around the World","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about food around the world and 4 Tier 2 words (differ, distinguish, feature, origin)","recognize and use food and cooking collocations (boil water, add salt, a pinch of salt, fresh bread)","choose the natural word partner instead of a direct translation"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن الطعام حول العالم و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","التعرّف على المتلازمات اللفظية في الطعام والطبخ واستخدامها.","اختيار الكلمة المرافقة الطبيعية بدل الترجمة الحرفية."],"success_criteria":["I can use the food words correctly in sentences.","I can match cooking verbs, adjectives and amount words with their usual partners.","I can correct an unnatural collocation such as *make the table* → *set the table*."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات الطعام في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع مطابقة أفعال الطبخ والصفات وكلمات الكميات مع مرافقاتها المعتادة.","أستطيع تصحيح متلازمة غير طبيعية مثل make the table إلى set the table."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"cuisine","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kwɪˈzin/","definition":"the style of cooking of a country or a region","arabic":"مطبخ (أسلوب طهي)","example":"Saudi cuisine is famous for its rice dishes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"recipe","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈrɛsəpi/","definition":"instructions for cooking a dish","arabic":"وصفة طبخ","example":"Follow the recipe step by step.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"ingredient","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪnˈɡridiənt/","definition":"one of the foods you use to make a dish","arabic":"مكوّن","example":"Rice is the main ingredient of kabsa.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"spice","pos":"noun","ipa":"/spaɪs/","definition":"a dried part of a plant used to add flavor, such as cardamom or cumin","arabic":"بهار / تابل","example":"Cardamom is a popular spice in Saudi Arabia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"flavor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfleɪvər/","definition":"the taste of food or drink","arabic":"نكهة","example":"Dried lime gives kabsa a sour flavor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"dish","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪʃ/","definition":"a type of food prepared in a particular way; also a plate","arabic":"طبق","example":"Paella is a famous Spanish dish.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"traditional","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/trəˈdɪʃənəl/","definition":"done in the same way for a very long time","arabic":"تقليدي","example":"Jareesh is a traditional Saudi dish.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"harvest","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ˈhɑrvɪst/","definition":"to collect crops when they are ready; the time of collecting crops","arabic":"يحصد / حصاد","example":"Farmers harvest dates in late summer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"grain","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɡreɪn/","definition":"the seed of plants such as rice, wheat and barley","arabic":"حبوب","example":"Rice is the grain that feeds the world.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"bake","pos":"verb","ipa":"/beɪk/","definition":"to cook food in an oven with dry heat","arabic":"يخبز","example":"We bake bread in the oven.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"grill","pos":"verb / noun","ipa":"/ɡrɪl/","definition":"to cook food over or under strong direct heat","arabic":"يشوي","example":"Grill the chicken until it is brown.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"portion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɔrʃən/","definition":"the amount of food for one person","arabic":"حصّة","example":"A portion of rice is about one cup.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"nutritious","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/nuˈtrɪʃəs/","definition":"full of things the body needs to be healthy","arabic":"مغذٍّ","example":"Brown rice is more nutritious than white rice.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"crop","pos":"noun","ipa":"/krɑp/","definition":"a plant grown by farmers for food","arabic":"محصول","example":"Rice is the most important crop in Asia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"differ","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈdɪfər/","definition":"to be not the same as something else","arabic":"يختلف","example":"Rice dishes differ from country to country.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"distinguish","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈstɪŋɡwɪʃ/","definition":"to see or show the difference between two things","arabic":"يميّز","example":"Can you distinguish basmati from jasmine rice?","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"feature","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfitʃər/","definition":"an important or typical part of something","arabic":"سمة / ميزة","example":"Saffron is a key feature of paella.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"origin","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɔrədʒɪn/","definition":"the place where something started","arabic":"أصل / منشأ","example":"Mandi has its origin in Yemen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **collocation** is a group of words that **naturally go together** in English. Native speakers say ***boil water*** and ***set the table***, not *cook water* or *make the table*. Learning words in their **partner groups** makes your English sound natural – and helps you follow recipes.\n\nCommon patterns: **verb + noun** – *boil water, add salt, chop onions, follow a recipe, set the table, wash the dishes*; **adjective + noun** – *fresh bread, strong flavor, a balanced diet, a heavy meal, a light snack*; **verb + adverb** – *stir gently, cook slowly, mix well*; **amount + of + noun** – *a pinch of salt, a slice of bread, a clove of garlic, a bunch of parsley, a cup of rice*.\n\nTip: Arabic and English don't always use the same partner. Arabic speakers may say *make a mistake* correctly, but *do a cake* is wrong – we ***make*** or ***bake*** a cake. When you learn a food word, write down **two or three of its partners**.","examples":["verb + noun: ***Boil the water*** before you ***add the rice***.","verb + noun: ***Chop the onions*** and ***crush the garlic***.","verb + noun: ***Follow the recipe*** carefully and ***set the table*** for six.","adjective + noun: ***Fresh bread*** from the bakery smells wonderful.","adjective + noun: Dried lime gives kabsa a ***strong flavor***.","verb + adverb: ***Stir the sauce gently*** so it doesn't stick.","amount: ***a pinch of salt***, ***a clove of garlic***, ***a slice of bread***.","amount: ***a bunch of parsley***, ***two cups of rice***, ***a handful of almonds***.","Unnatural ✗ → natural ✓: *make the table* → ***set the table***; *strong rain* → ***heavy rain***; *do a meal* → ***cook / make a meal***."],"common_errors":["✗ Make the table, please. → ✓ Set the table, please. (set the table.)","✗ Cook the water first. → ✓ Boil the water first. (boil water.)","✗ Put a little salt piece. → ✓ Add a pinch of salt. (a pinch of salt.)","✗ This soup has a big flavor. → ✓ This soup has a strong flavor. (strong flavor.)","✗ Open the oven to 180 degrees. → ✓ Preheat the oven to 180 degrees. (preheat / turn on the oven.)","✗ Two rices, please. → ✓ Two portions of rice, please. (rice = uncountable.)","✗ We eat a heavy food at lunch. → ✓ We eat a heavy meal at lunch. (a heavy meal.)"],"arabic":"المتلازمة اللفظية مجموعة كلمات تأتي معًا بشكل طبيعي في الإنجليزية، فيقال boil water وset the table، لا cook water أو make the table.\n\nأنماط شائعة: فعل + اسم (boil water, add salt, chop onions)، وصفة + اسم (fresh bread, strong flavor)، وفعل + ظرف (stir gently)، وكمية + of + اسم (a pinch of salt, a clove of garlic).\n\nلا تترجم حرفيًا من العربية؛ فنقول make/bake a cake لا do a cake. وعندما تتعلم كلمة طعام، اكتب معها كلمتين أو ثلاثًا من مرافقاتها.\n\nانتبه: rice اسم غير معدود، فنقول two portions of rice أو two cups of rice."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"From kabsa in Riyadh to sushi in Tokyo – welcome to a world of flavors!","on_screen":"Food Around the World 🍚🌍","visual":"A spinning globe with dishes popping up on each country."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Cuisine, recipe, ingredient, spice, flavor, dish, traditional, harvest, grain, bake, grill, portion, nutritious, crop!","on_screen":"cuisine · recipe · ingredient · spice · flavor · dish · traditional · harvest · grain · bake · grill · portion · nutritious · crop","visual":"Each word appears on a recipe card with a picture."},{"time":"01:30–02:05","narration":"Dishes differ from place to place. Can you distinguish their features and their origin?","on_screen":"differ · distinguish · feature · origin","visual":"Two rice dishes side by side with labels."},{"time":"02:05–03:30","narration":"Boil water. Add salt. Chop onions. Stir gently. A pinch of salt, a clove of garlic – words that cook together!","on_screen":"verb + noun · adjective + noun · verb + adverb · amount + of","visual":"Word pairs jump into a cooking pot."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Don't make the table – SET the table! Learn words with their partners.","on_screen":"make the table ✗ → set the table ✓","visual":"A table sets itself with plates and cups."}],"captions_ar":["من الكبسة في الرياض إلى السوشي في طوكيو، مرحبًا بك في عالم النكهات!","مطبخ، وصفة، مكوّن، بهار، نكهة، طبق، تقليدي، حصاد، حبوب، يخبز، يشوي، حصة، مغذٍّ، محصول!","تختلف الأطباق من مكان إلى آخر. هل تستطيع تمييز سماتها وأصلها؟","اغلِ الماء، أضف الملح، قطّع البصل، حرّك برفق، رشة ملح، فص ثوم: كلمات تطبخ معًا!","لا تقل make the table بل set the table! تعلّم الكلمات مع مرافقاتها."],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm male chef-style voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct food word.","instructions_ar":"اختر كلمة الطعام الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"Onions, tomatoes, chicken and rice are the main ___ of kabsa.","answer":"ingredients","explanation":"Ingredient.","explanation_ar":"مكوّنات.","options":["cuisines","crops","portions","ingredients"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"My grandmother never writes down her ___ – she keeps every step in her head.","answer":"recipes","explanation":"Recipe.","explanation_ar":"وصفاتها.","options":["grains","harvests","recipes","features"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Japanese ___ includes sushi, miso soup and tempura.","answer":"cuisine","explanation":"Cuisine.","explanation_ar":"المطبخ الياباني.","options":["portion","crop","origin","cuisine"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Wheat, rice and barley are kinds of ___.","answer":"grain","explanation":"Grain.","explanation_ar":"حبوب.","options":["spice","grain","dish","flavor"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"We ___ the bread in a very hot oven for twenty minutes.","answer":"bake","explanation":"Bake.","explanation_ar":"نخبز.","options":["bake","grill","harvest","boil"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Lentils, vegetables and fish make a ___ meal that keeps you healthy.","answer":"nutritious","explanation":"Nutritious.","explanation_ar":"مغذية.","options":["traditional","heavy","nutritious","dried"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Collocations. Choose the natural partner.","instructions_ar":"المتلازمات: اختر المرافق الطبيعي.","items":[{"q":"Before guests arrive, please ___ the table.","answer":"set","explanation":"set the table.","explanation_ar":"set.","options":["set","make","do","put"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"___ the water before you add the pasta.","answer":"Boil","explanation":"boil water.","explanation_ar":"Boil.","options":["Cook","Bake","Grill","Boil"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Add a ___ of salt to the soup.","answer":"pinch","explanation":"a pinch of salt.","explanation_ar":"رشة.","options":["slice","bunch","pinch","loaf"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Dried lime gives the rice a ___ flavor.","answer":"strong","explanation":"strong flavor.","explanation_ar":"قوية.","options":["big","strong","heavy","tall"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which collocation for a kitchen job is natural?","answer":"do the dishes","explanation":"do the dishes.","explanation_ar":"do the dishes.","options":["make the dishes","do the dishes","set the dishes","boil the dishes"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about cooking rice uses a natural collocation?","answer":"Stir the rice gently so the grains don't break.","explanation":"stir gently.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Stir the rice gently so the grains don't break.","Stir the rice strongly so the grains don't break.","Make the rice gently so the grains don't break.","Do the rice gently so the grains don't break."],"dok":3,"skill":"collocations","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank. Each word fits one sentence.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Rice dishes ________ a lot from one country to another.","answer":"differ","explanation":"Are not the same.","explanation_ar":"تختلف.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Can you ________ the smell of cardamom from the smell of cinnamon?","answer":"distinguish","explanation":"Tell the difference.","explanation_ar":"تميّز.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"A wide, flat pan is a typical ________ of Spanish paella.","answer":"feature","explanation":"Typical part.","explanation_ar":"سمة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Pizza has its ________ in Naples, Italy.","answer":"origin","explanation":"Starting place.","explanation_ar":"أصل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Chop the parsley and crush a ________ of garlic.","answer":"clove","explanation":"Amount word.","explanation_ar":"فص.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The farmers will ________ the wheat in early summer.","answer":"harvest","explanation":"Collect crops.","explanation_ar":"يحصدون.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words.","instructions_ar":"اكتب باستخدام الكلمات الجديدة.","items":[{"q":"Write four cooking instructions for a simple dish using collocations.","answer":"Boil the water. Add a pinch of salt. Chop the onions. Stir the sauce gently.","explanation":"Collocations.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمات.","dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Correct the unnatural collocations: *Make the table and cook the water.*","answer":"Set the table and boil the water.","explanation":"Correct.","explanation_ar":"صحّح.","dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Describe a traditional dish from your family. Use *ingredient*, *spice* and *flavor*.","answer":"My grandmother's jareesh is a traditional dish. Its main ingredient is crushed wheat, and she adds cumin as a spice, which gives it a warm flavor.","explanation":"Describe.","explanation_ar":"صِف.","dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"production"},{"q":"How do Saudi and Japanese rice dishes differ? Give one difference.","answer":"Saudi rice dishes like kabsa use long-grain rice and many spices, but Japanese sushi uses short, sticky rice with vinegar.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"comparing","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Collocation Kitchen","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Kitchen game: ingredients (word halves) fall onto a counter; the child drags matching halves into the pot to make a natural collocation. Wrong pairs make the pot smoke! Five correct pairs complete a dish.","Each team draws six verb/adjective/amount cards.","They find the matching noun cards on the table.","They put each pair in the pot and say a cooking sentence with it.","Other teams can challenge unnatural pairs.","Natural pair = 1 point; correct sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *the style of cooking of a country*?","answer":"cuisine","explanation":"Cuisine.","explanation_ar":"المطبخ.","options":["crop","portion","cuisine","grain"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What is a *collocation*?","answer":"words that naturally go together","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة لفظية.","options":["a word with the opposite meaning","a kind of spice","a list of ingredients","words that naturally go together"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"*Brown rice is more **nutritious** than white rice.* *Nutritious* means ___.","answer":"full of things the body needs","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"مغذٍّ.","options":["more expensive","full of things the body needs","darker in color","harder to cook"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which collocation is natural?","answer":"a slice of bread","explanation":"Amount.","explanation_ar":"شريحة خبز.","options":["a pinch of bread","a clove of bread","a slice of bread","a bunch of bread"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Which sentence for ordering rice at a restaurant is correct?","answer":"Can I have two portions of rice, please?","explanation":"Uncountable.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Can I have two portions of rice, please?","Can I have two rices, please?","Can I have two rice, please?","Can I have a two rice, please?"],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a natural collocation?","answer":"Please follow the recipe carefully.","explanation":"follow a recipe.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Please make the recipe carefully.","Please follow the recipe carefully.","Please do the recipe carefully.","Please set the recipe carefully."],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"*Mandi has its **origin** in Yemen.* *Origin* means ___.","answer":"the place where something started","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"الأصل.","options":["the place where something started","the main ingredient","the cooking time","the price"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which adjective is the natural partner of *meal* when you feel very full after eating?","answer":"heavy","explanation":"heavy meal.","explanation_ar":"heavy.","options":["strong","thick","heavy","big-tall"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"A student writes *Do a cake for the party.* What is the BEST correction?","answer":"Make (or bake) a cake for the party.","explanation":"Collocation.","explanation_ar":"make/bake a cake.","options":["Set a cake for the party.","Boil a cake for the party.","Cook up a cake party.","Make (or bake) a cake for the party."],"dok":3,"skill":"collocations"},{"q":"Why is it helpful to learn food words together with their partners?","answer":"It makes your English sound natural and helps you understand recipes.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"يجعل اللغة طبيعية.","options":["It makes words shorter.","It helps you skip new words.","Partners are only used in poems.","It makes your English sound natural and helps you understand recipes."],"dok":3,"skill":"word strategies"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'cuisine': a table with dishes from one country"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'recipe': a recipe card with a list of steps"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ingredient': rice, onions, tomatoes and spices on a counter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'spice': small bowls of colorful spices"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'flavor': a child tasting a spoon of food"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'dish': a large pan of yellow rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'traditional': a grandmother cooking in a clay pot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'harvest': farmers collecting dates from a palm"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'grain': rice, wheat and barley grains in bowls"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bake': loaves of bread in an oven"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'grill': chicken pieces on a grill"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'portion': a plate with one serving of rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'nutritious': a plate with rice, vegetables and fish"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crop': green rice plants in a flooded field"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'differ': two different plates side by side"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'distinguish': a magnifying glass over two kinds of rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'feature': a red saffron thread highlighted on a pan"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'origin': a map pin on a country"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-01","prompt":"Children sharing rice dishes from around the world."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-02","prompt":"Icons for food words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS1-03","prompt":"Word pairs rising from a cooking pot."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V01","script":"cuisine. Saudi cuisine is famous for its rice dishes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V02","script":"recipe. Follow the recipe step by step."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V03","script":"ingredient. Rice is the main ingredient of kabsa."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V04","script":"spice. Cardamom is a popular spice in Saudi Arabia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V05","script":"flavor. Dried lime gives kabsa a sour flavor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V06","script":"dish. Paella is a famous Spanish dish."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V07","script":"traditional. Jareesh is a traditional Saudi dish."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V08","script":"harvest. Farmers harvest dates in late summer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V09","script":"grain. Rice is the grain that feeds the world."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V10","script":"bake. We bake bread in the oven."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V11","script":"grill. Grill the chicken until it is brown."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V12","script":"portion. A portion of rice is about one cup."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V13","script":"nutritious. Brown rice is more nutritious than white rice."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V14","script":"crop. Rice is the most important crop in Asia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V15","script":"differ. Rice dishes differ from country to country."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V16","script":"distinguish. Can you distinguish basmati from jasmine rice?"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V17","script":"feature. Saffron is a key feature of paella."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-V18","script":"origin. Mandi has its origin in Yemen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS1-VID","script":"From kabsa in Riyadh to sushi in Tokyo – welcome to a world of flavors! Cuisine, recipe, ingredient, spice, flavor, dish, traditional, harvest, grain, bake, grill, portion, nutritious, crop! Dishes differ from place to place. Can you distinguish their features and their origin? Boil water. Add salt. Chop onions. Stir gently. A pinch of salt, a clove of garlic – words that cook together! Don't make the table – SET the table! Learn words with their partners."}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2","title":"Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read an informational article of about 460 words about rice around the world","recognize the text structure of each section: description, sequence, compare and contrast, and problem and solution","use signal words and graphic organizers to explain how each structure organizes the information"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة مقال معلوماتي من نحو 460 كلمة عن الأرز حول العالم.","التعرّف على بنية كل قسم: الوصف والتسلسل والمقارنة والمشكلة والحل.","استخدام الكلمات الدالة والمنظِّمات لشرح كيف تنظم كل بنية المعلومات."],"success_criteria":["I can name the structure of each section and the signal words that show it.","I can retell the steps from seed to bowl in order.","I can explain one similarity, one difference and one problem with its solution from the article."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تسمية بنية كل قسم والكلمات الدالة عليها.","أستطيع إعادة سرد خطوات زراعة الأرز بالترتيب.","أستطيع شرح تشابه واختلاف ومشكلة مع حلها من المقال."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"paddy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpædi/","definition":"a flooded field where rice is grown","arabic":"حقل أرز مغمور","example":"Farmers plant rice in paddies.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"tame","pos":"verb","ipa":"/teɪm/","definition":"to make a wild plant or animal useful for people","arabic":"يستأنس / يروّض","example":"People tamed wild rice thousands of years ago.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"drain","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dreɪn/","definition":"to let water flow away from something","arabic":"يصرّف (الماء)","example":"Farmers drain the water before the harvest.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"terrace","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɛrəs/","definition":"a flat area cut into a hill, like a step","arabic":"مدرّج زراعي","example":"The terraces rise like green steps.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"bran","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bræn/","definition":"the brown outer layer of a grain","arabic":"النخالة","example":"Brown rice keeps its bran.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"fiber","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfaɪbər/","definition":"the part of food that helps your body digest it","arabic":"ألياف","example":"Brown rice has more fiber than white rice.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"methane","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛθeɪn/","definition":"a gas that traps heat and warms the planet","arabic":"غاز الميثان","example":"Flooded paddies release methane.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"Writers organize information in different **text structures**. One article can use **several**, one in each section. Knowing the structure helps you **predict**, **understand** and **remember** the information.\n\nFour common structures and their **signal words**: (1) **Description** – gives features or facts about one topic (*for example, such as, is*); (2) **Sequence** – steps or events in order (*first, next, after, finally*); (3) **Compare and contrast** – similarities and differences (*both, same, but, different, while, however*); (4) **Problem and solution** – a problem and how people fix it (*problem, to solve this, so, however*).\n\nTo find the structure: (1) read the **heading**; (2) look for **signal words**; (3) ask **what the section does** – describe, list steps, compare or solve? (4) choose an **organizer**: a web for description, a flow chart for sequence, a Venn-style chart for comparison, and a problem → solution chart.","examples":["Description – *A Very Old Crop*: facts about rice's history (*at least 8,000 years ago … about 90 percent*).","Sequence – *From Seed to Bowl*: ***First*** plant → ***Next*** keep fields flooded → ***After*** three to six months drain → ***Finally*** harvest and dry.","Compare and contrast – *White or Brown?*: ***Both*** come from the same plant, ***but*** brown rice keeps its bran…","Contrast detail: brown – *chewier, more fiber*; white – *cooks faster, stored for longer*.","Description with examples – *One Grain, Many Dishes*: biryani, paella, sushi, jollof, kabsa.","Problem and solution – *A Thirsty Crop*: problem – *needs a lot of water … release methane*; solution – ***To solve this***, *flood and dry their fields in turns*.","Signal word *however*: *Rice has a problem, **however**: it needs a lot of water.*","Signal word *while*: *Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice …, **while** in West Africa, families share jollof rice.*","Conclusion – the last sentence connects the whole article: *this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day.*"],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking an article has only one structure. → ✓ Check each section – structures can change. (Section by section.)","✗ Calling every list a sequence. → ✓ Sequence = order matters (first, next, finally). (Order matters?)","✗ Missing *but* and *while* as contrast signals. → ✓ *but, while, however* show differences. (Contrast signals.)","✗ Brown rice and white rice is the same. → ✓ Brown rice and white rice are similar, but they are different in important ways. (Plural → **are**.)","✗ Rice is grown in every continents. → ✓ Rice is grown on every continent except Antarctica. (every + singular; **on** a continent.)","✗ Writing the problem without the solution. → ✓ Find *To solve this…* and write both. (Problem → solution.)","✗ Using the wrong organizer. → ✓ Flow chart for sequence; comparison chart for compare/contrast. (Match the organizer.)"],"arabic":"ينظّم الكتّاب المعلومات في بنى نصية مختلفة، وقد يستخدم المقال الواحد عدة بنى، لكل قسم بنيته. ومعرفة البنية تساعدك على التوقع والفهم والتذكر.\n\nأربع بنى شائعة: الوصف (for example, such as)، والتسلسل (first, next, finally)، والمقارنة (both, but, while, however)، والمشكلة والحل (problem, to solve this).\n\nلتحديد البنية: اقرأ العنوان، وابحث عن الكلمات الدالة، واسأل: ماذا يفعل القسم؟ ثم اختر المنظّم المناسب.\n\nانتبه: نقول on every continent بالمفرد، وbrown rice and white rice are."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Riyadh, Tokyo, Lagos – what do their dinner tables share? Rice!","on_screen":"Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World 🍚","visual":"Three family meals in three countries, all with rice."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"First, farmers plant young rice plants. Next, they flood the fields. After three to six months, they drain the water. Finally, they harvest.","on_screen":"Sequence: First → Next → After → Finally","visual":"Animated paddy fields change from green to gold; terraces in the Philippines."},{"time":"01:40–02:30","narration":"Brown rice keeps its bran, but white rice cooks faster. Both come from the same plant!","on_screen":"Compare and contrast","visual":"A split screen: brown grain vs. white grain with labels."},{"time":"02:30–03:20","narration":"Rice needs a lot of water. To solve this, farmers flood and dry their fields in turns.","on_screen":"Problem → Solution","visual":"A field filling and drying; methane bubbles decreasing."},{"time":"03:20–04:00","narration":"Description, sequence, compare and contrast, problem and solution – four structures, one article!","on_screen":"4 text structures","visual":"Four organizers appear: web, flow chart, comparison chart, problem-solution arrows."}],"captions_ar":["الرياض وطوكيو ولاغوس: ما الذي تشترك فيه موائدها؟ الأرز!","أولًا يزرع المزارعون الشتلات، ثم يغمرون الحقول، وبعد ثلاثة إلى ستة أشهر يصرّفون الماء، وأخيرًا يحصدون.","الأرز البني يحتفظ بنخالته، أما الأبيض فيُطهى أسرع، وكلاهما من النبات نفسه!","يحتاج الأرز إلى ماء كثير، ولحل ذلك يغمر المزارعون حقولهم ويجففونها بالتناوب.","الوصف والتسلسل والمقارنة والمشكلة والحل: أربع بنى في مقال واحد!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female documentary voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World","body":"What do a family in Riyadh, a fisherman in Japan and a farmer in Nigeria have in common? Very likely, they will all eat rice today. Rice is the main food of more than half of the people on Earth, and it is grown on every continent except Antarctica.\n\n**A Very Old Crop**\n\nPeople have been growing rice for thousands of years. Scientists have found evidence that farmers in China were growing rice at least 8,000 years ago. In West Africa, people tamed a different kind of rice on their own about 3,000 years ago. Today, about 90 percent of the world's rice is grown and eaten in Asia.\n\n**From Seed to Bowl**\n\nMost rice grows in flooded fields called paddies. First, farmers plant young rice plants in the wet soil. Next, they keep the fields covered with water, which helps stop weeds from growing. After three to six months, the plants turn golden, and the farmers drain the water. Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells. In the mountains of the Philippines, farmers have carved rice terraces that rise up the hills like giant green steps.\n\n**White or Brown?**\n\nBrown rice and white rice come from the same plant, but they are different in important ways. Brown rice keeps its outer layer, called the bran, so it is chewier, takes longer to cook and has more fiber and vitamins. White rice has the bran removed. It cooks faster, it is softer, and it can be stored for longer, which is why many people prefer it.\n\n**One Grain, Many Dishes**\n\nThere are thousands of kinds of rice. Long, thin basmati rice from India and Pakistan is the favorite in Saudi kitchens, where it becomes kabsa, mandi and many other family favorites. In India and Pakistan, cooks layer rice with spiced meat to make biryani. In Spain, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred. Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice mixed with vinegar to make sushi, while in West Africa, families share jollof rice cooked in a rich tomato and pepper sauce. Saudi Arabia even grows its own traditional red rice, called Hassawi rice, in the Al-Ahsa Oasis.\n\n**A Thirsty Crop**\n\nRice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water. Flooded paddies also release methane, a gas that warms the planet. To solve this, scientists are teaching farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns, which saves water and produces less methane.\n\nFrom a small bowl of sushi in Tokyo to a huge tray of kabsa shared by a Saudi family, this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day.","word_count":454,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS2-1","genre":"informational","role":"main","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"paddy","definition":"a flooded field where rice is grown","arabic":"حقل أرز مغمور"},{"word":"bran","definition":"the brown outer layer of a grain","arabic":"النخالة"},{"word":"methane","definition":"a gas that traps heat and warms the planet","arabic":"غاز الميثان"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the article. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"On which continent is rice NOT grown?","answer":"Antarctica","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"القارة القطبية الجنوبية.","options":["Antarctica","Asia","Africa","Europe"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long ago were farmers in China growing rice?","answer":"at least 8,000 years ago","explanation":"A Very Old Crop.","explanation_ar":"منذ 8000 عام على الأقل.","options":["at least 8,000 years ago","about 800 years ago","about 80 years ago","about 3,000 years ago"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why do farmers keep paddies covered with water?","answer":"It helps stop weeds from growing.","explanation":"From Seed to Bowl.","explanation_ar":"لمنع الأعشاب الضارة.","options":["It makes the rice white.","It helps stop weeds from growing.","It keeps birds away.","It makes the rice sticky."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is *bran*?","answer":"the outer layer of a grain","explanation":"White or Brown?","explanation_ar":"النخالة.","options":["a kind of sauce","the outer layer of a grain","a rice field","a cooking pan"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which rice is grown in the Al-Ahsa Oasis?","answer":"Hassawi red rice","explanation":"One Grain, Many Dishes.","explanation_ar":"الأرز الحساوي.","options":["basmati rice","jasmine rice","Hassawi red rice","sticky sushi rice"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which gas do flooded paddies release?","answer":"methane","explanation":"A Thirsty Crop.","explanation_ar":"الميثان.","options":["oxygen","carbon monoxide","steam only","methane"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Text structures. Choose.","instructions_ar":"البنى النصية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What is the text structure of *From Seed to Bowl*?","answer":"sequence","explanation":"Signal words: first, next, finally.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل.","options":["compare and contrast","problem and solution","sequence","description only"],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which signal words show that *White or Brown?* is a compare-and-contrast section?","answer":"same plant, but, different","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"same, but, different.","options":["first, next, finally","to solve this","for example, such as","same plant, but, different"],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In *A Thirsty Crop*, what is the SOLUTION?","answer":"Farmers flood and dry their fields in turns.","explanation":"Problem → solution.","explanation_ar":"الغمر والتجفيف بالتناوب.","options":["Farmers stop growing rice.","Farmers flood and dry their fields in turns.","Farmers use more water.","Farmers grow rice in deserts."],"dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What happens right AFTER the plants turn golden?","answer":"The farmers drain the water.","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"يصرّف المزارعون الماء.","options":["The farmers plant young rice plants.","The farmers flood the fields.","The farmers drain the water.","The farmers cook kabsa."],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why might many people prefer white rice to brown rice?","answer":"It cooks faster, is softer and can be stored longer.","explanation":"Contrast.","explanation_ar":"أسرع طهيًا وأطول تخزينًا.","options":["It has more fiber.","It cooks faster, is softer and can be stored longer.","It keeps its bran.","It is chewier."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which organizer BEST fits *A Thirsty Crop*?","answer":"a problem → solution chart","explanation":"Organizer.","explanation_ar":"مخطط المشكلة والحل.","options":["a problem → solution chart","a timeline of dates","a word web of spices","a Venn diagram of two dishes"],"dok":3,"skill":"text structure","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that introduces the problem with rice.","answer":"Rice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water.","explanation":"Problem.","explanation_ar":"المشكلة.","evidence":"Rice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"freer","sentences":["What do a family in Riyadh, a fisherman in Japan and a farmer in Nigeria have in common?","Very likely, they will all eat rice today.","Rice is the main food of more than half of the people on Earth, and it is grown on every continent except Antarctica.","**A Very Old Crop**","People have been growing rice for thousands of years.","Scientists have found evidence that farmers in China were growing rice at least 8,000 years ago.","In West Africa, people tamed a different kind of rice on their own about 3,000 years ago.","Today, about 90 percent of the world's rice is grown and eaten in Asia.","**From Seed to Bowl**","Most rice grows in flooded fields called paddies.","First, farmers plant young rice plants in the wet soil.","Next, they keep the fields covered with water, which helps stop weeds from growing.","After three to six months, the plants turn golden, and the farmers drain the water.","Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells.","In the mountains of the Philippines, farmers have carved rice terraces that rise up the hills like giant green steps.","**White or Brown?**","Brown rice and white rice come from the same plant, but they are different in important ways.","Brown rice keeps its outer layer, called the bran, so it is chewier, takes longer to cook and has more fiber and vitamins.","White rice has the bran removed.","It cooks faster, it is softer, and it can be stored for longer, which is why many people prefer it.","**One Grain, Many Dishes**","There are thousands of kinds of rice.","Long, thin basmati rice from India and Pakistan is the favorite in Saudi kitchens, where it becomes kabsa, mandi and many other family favorites.","In India and Pakistan, cooks layer rice with spiced meat to make biryani.","In Spain, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred.","Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice mixed with vinegar to make sushi, while in West Africa, families share jollof rice cooked in a rich tomato and pepper sauce.","Saudi Arabia even grows its own traditional red rice, called Hassawi rice, in the Al-Ahsa Oasis.","**A Thirsty Crop**","Rice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water.","Flooded paddies also release methane, a gas that warms the planet.","To solve this, scientists are teaching farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns, which saves water and produces less methane.","From a small bowl of sushi in Tokyo to a huge tray of kabsa shared by a Saudi family, this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that gives the final step in growing rice.","answer":"Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells.","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل.","evidence":"Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"freer","sentences":["What do a family in Riyadh, a fisherman in Japan and a farmer in Nigeria have in common?","Very likely, they will all eat rice today.","Rice is the main food of more than half of the people on Earth, and it is grown on every continent except Antarctica.","**A Very Old Crop**","People have been growing rice for thousands of years.","Scientists have found evidence that farmers in China were growing rice at least 8,000 years ago.","In West Africa, people tamed a different kind of rice on their own about 3,000 years ago.","Today, about 90 percent of the world's rice is grown and eaten in Asia.","**From Seed to Bowl**","Most rice grows in flooded fields called paddies.","First, farmers plant young rice plants in the wet soil.","Next, they keep the fields covered with water, which helps stop weeds from growing.","After three to six months, the plants turn golden, and the farmers drain the water.","Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells.","In the mountains of the Philippines, farmers have carved rice terraces that rise up the hills like giant green steps.","**White or Brown?**","Brown rice and white rice come from the same plant, but they are different in important ways.","Brown rice keeps its outer layer, called the bran, so it is chewier, takes longer to cook and has more fiber and vitamins.","White rice has the bran removed.","It cooks faster, it is softer, and it can be stored for longer, which is why many people prefer it.","**One Grain, Many Dishes**","There are thousands of kinds of rice.","Long, thin basmati rice from India and Pakistan is the favorite in Saudi kitchens, where it becomes kabsa, mandi and many other family favorites.","In India and Pakistan, cooks layer rice with spiced meat to make biryani.","In Spain, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred.","Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice mixed with vinegar to make sushi, while in West Africa, families share jollof rice cooked in a rich tomato and pepper sauce.","Saudi Arabia even grows its own traditional red rice, called Hassawi rice, in the Al-Ahsa Oasis.","**A Thirsty Crop**","Rice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water.","Flooded paddies also release methane, a gas that warms the planet.","To solve this, scientists are teaching farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns, which saves water and produces less methane.","From a small bowl of sushi in Tokyo to a huge tray of kabsa shared by a Saudi family, this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day."]},{"q":"Why does the author begin with a question about Riyadh, Japan and Nigeria?","answer":"to hook readers and show that rice is eaten all over the world","explanation":"Author's craft.","explanation_ar":"لجذب القارئ وإظهار انتشار الأرز.","options":["to hook readers and show that rice is eaten all over the world","to give a recipe","to compare three cities' weather","to describe how rice grows"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"},{"q":"What is the main idea of the WHOLE rice article?","answer":"Rice is an ancient, important food grown and cooked in many ways that connects people around the world.","explanation":"Main idea.","explanation_ar":"الفكرة الرئيسية.","options":["Brown rice has more fiber.","Paella is cooked in a flat pan.","Paddies release methane.","Rice is an ancient, important food grown and cooked in many ways that connects people around the world."],"dok":3,"skill":"main idea","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain the structures.","instructions_ar":"اشرح البنى.","items":[{"q":"Retell how rice grows from seed to bowl, using four sequence words.","answer":"First, farmers plant young rice plants in wet soil. Next, they keep the fields flooded. After three to six months, they drain the water. Finally, they harvest, dry and shell the grains.","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"التسلسل.","dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one similarity and two differences between brown rice and white rice.","answer":"Both come from the same plant. Brown rice has more fiber and vitamins, but white rice cooks faster and can be stored longer.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the problem with growing rice and the solution scientists suggest.","answer":"Rice needs a lot of water, and flooded paddies release methane, which warms the planet. Scientists teach farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns to save water and make less methane.","explanation":"Problem → solution.","explanation_ar":"مشكلة وحل.","dok":3,"skill":"problem and solution","stage":"production"},{"q":"Choose two rice dishes from the article and explain one way they differ.","answer":"Paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan and is not stirred, while biryani is made by layering rice with spiced meat.","explanation":"Contrast.","explanation_ar":"باين.","dok":3,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World”.","answer":"454 words","title":"Rice: The Grain That Feeds the World","body":"What do a family in Riyadh, a fisherman in Japan and a farmer in Nigeria have in common? Very likely, they will all eat rice today. Rice is the main food of more than half of the people on Earth, and it is grown on every continent except Antarctica.\n\n**A Very Old Crop**\n\nPeople have been growing rice for thousands of years. Scientists have found evidence that farmers in China were growing rice at least 8,000 years ago. In West Africa, people tamed a different kind of rice on their own about 3,000 years ago. Today, about 90 percent of the world's rice is grown and eaten in Asia.\n\n**From Seed to Bowl**\n\nMost rice grows in flooded fields called paddies. First, farmers plant young rice plants in the wet soil. Next, they keep the fields covered with water, which helps stop weeds from growing. After three to six months, the plants turn golden, and the farmers drain the water. Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells. In the mountains of the Philippines, farmers have carved rice terraces that rise up the hills like giant green steps.\n\n**White or Brown?**\n\nBrown rice and white rice come from the same plant, but they are different in important ways. Brown rice keeps its outer layer, called the bran, so it is chewier, takes longer to cook and has more fiber and vitamins. White rice has the bran removed. It cooks faster, it is softer, and it can be stored for longer, which is why many people prefer it.\n\n**One Grain, Many Dishes**\n\nThere are thousands of kinds of rice. Long, thin basmati rice from India and Pakistan is the favorite in Saudi kitchens, where it becomes kabsa, mandi and many other family favorites. In India and Pakistan, cooks layer rice with spiced meat to make biryani. In Spain, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred. Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice mixed with vinegar to make sushi, while in West Africa, families share jollof rice cooked in a rich tomato and pepper sauce. Saudi Arabia even grows its own traditional red rice, called Hassawi rice, in the Al-Ahsa Oasis.\n\n**A Thirsty Crop**\n\nRice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water. Flooded paddies also release methane, a gas that warms the planet. To solve this, scientists are teaching farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns, which saves water and produces less methane.\n\nFrom a small bowl of sushi in Tokyo to a huge tray of kabsa shared by a Saudi family, this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day.","words":454,"limit":251}],"time_limit_sec":251},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS2-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Text Structure Map","instructions":"Organizer: For each section, write the structure, the signal words and the key information.","items":[{"q":"A Very Old Crop – structure + key facts","answer":"Description: 8,000+ years in China; about 3,000 years in West Africa; 90% grown and eaten in Asia"},{"q":"From Seed to Bowl – structure + steps in order","answer":"Sequence: plant → keep flooded → after 3–6 months drain → harvest, dry, remove shells"},{"q":"White or Brown? – structure + one similarity and two differences","answer":"Compare/contrast: both from the same plant; brown has bran, fiber, vitamins; white cooks faster and keeps longer"},{"q":"One Grain, Many Dishes – structure + examples","answer":"Description with examples: kabsa, biryani, paella, sushi, jollof, Hassawi rice"},{"q":"A Thirsty Crop – structure + problem and solution","answer":"Problem/solution: needs much water and releases methane → flood and dry fields in turns"},{"q":"How the conclusion connects the sections","answer":"Rice connects billions of people every day – the main idea of the whole article."}]}],"game":{"name":"Structure Chefs","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Structure kitchen: sentences drop onto a conveyor belt; the child sends each one to the right 'kitchen station' (description, sequence, compare/contrast, problem/solution) and arranges the sequence cards in order. Correct stations complete a rice dish.","Teams receive mixed sentence cards.","They place each card on the correct organizer mat.","On the sequence mat, cards must be in order.","Teams circle the signal words that helped them.","Correct mat = 1 point; correct order = 1 point per step."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *text structure*?","answer":"the way a writer organizes information","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"البنية النصية.","options":["the title of a text","the number of pages","the way a writer organizes information","a list of new words"],"dok":1,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"What is a *paddy*?","answer":"a flooded field where rice is grown","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"حقل أرز مغمور.","options":["a rice dish","a kind of spice","a flooded field where rice is grown","a cooking pot"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The farmers **drain** the water before the harvest.* *Drain* means ___.","answer":"let the water flow away","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يصرّفون.","options":["add more water","let the water flow away","boil the water","drink the water"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which signal words usually show SEQUENCE?","answer":"first, next, finally","explanation":"Signal words.","explanation_ar":"first, next, finally.","options":["first, next, finally","but, while, however","to solve this","for example"],"dok":1,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"Which sentence about where rice is grown is correct?","answer":"Rice is grown on every continent except Antarctica.","explanation":"Grammar.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Rice is grown in every continents except Antarctica.","Rice are grown on every continent except Antarctica.","Rice grown on every continent except Antarctica.","Rice is grown on every continent except Antarctica."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which section of the rice article uses COMPARE AND CONTRAST?","answer":"White or Brown?","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"الأبيض أم البني؟","options":["From Seed to Bowl","A Very Old Crop","A Thirsty Crop","White or Brown?"],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"Which word in *Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice…, while in West Africa…* shows a contrast?","answer":"while","explanation":"Signal.","explanation_ar":"while.","options":["cooks","while","sticky","use"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast"},{"q":"Why does it help to know the text structure of a section?","answer":"You can predict and organize the information, so you understand and remember it better.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"يساعد على الفهم والتذكر.","options":["You can skip the section.","You can predict and organize the information, so you understand and remember it better.","It tells you the author's name.","It makes the text shorter."],"dok":2,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"Which detail from the rice article would go in a PROBLEM box?","answer":"Flooded paddies release methane, a gas that warms the planet.","explanation":"Problem.","explanation_ar":"مشكلة.","options":["Farmers in China grew rice 8,000 years ago.","Paella is cooked in a flat pan.","Brown rice is chewier.","Flooded paddies release methane, a gas that warms the planet."],"dok":3,"skill":"problem and solution"},{"q":"How does the last sentence of the rice article connect the sections?","answer":"It links all the facts back to the main idea that rice connects people everywhere.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"يربط الأقسام بالفكرة الرئيسية.","options":["It links all the facts back to the main idea that rice connects people everywhere.","It adds a new recipe.","It gives a new problem.","It lists the steps again."],"dok":3,"skill":"main idea"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'paddy': a flooded green rice field"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tame': a farmer caring for young plants"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'drain': water flowing out of a field"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'terrace': rice terraces on a mountain"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'bran': a rice grain with a brown outer layer"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fiber': a bowl of brown rice and vegetables"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'methane': bubbles rising from a flooded field"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-01","prompt":"Rice terraces and flooded paddies."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-02","prompt":"A brown rice grain compared with a white rice grain."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS2-03","prompt":"Rice dishes on a world map."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V01","script":"paddy. Farmers plant rice in paddies."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V02","script":"tame. People tamed wild rice thousands of years ago."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V03","script":"drain. Farmers drain the water before the harvest."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V04","script":"terrace. The terraces rise like green steps."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V05","script":"bran. Brown rice keeps its bran."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V06","script":"fiber. Brown rice has more fiber than white rice."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-V07","script":"methane. Flooded paddies release methane."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-T1","script":"What do a family in Riyadh, a fisherman in Japan and a farmer in Nigeria have in common? Very likely, they will all eat rice today. Rice is the main food of more than half of the people on Earth, and it is grown on every continent except Antarctica. **A Very Old Crop** People have been growing rice for thousands of years. Scientists have found evidence that farmers in China were growing rice at least 8,000 years ago. In West Africa, people tamed a different kind of rice on their own about 3,000 years ago. Today, about 90 percent of the world's rice is grown and eaten in Asia. **From Seed to Bowl** Most rice grows in flooded fields called paddies. First, farmers plant young rice plants in the wet soil. Next, they keep the fields covered with water, which helps stop weeds from growing. After three to six months, the plants turn golden, and the farmers drain the water. Finally, they harvest the plants, dry the grains in the sun and remove the hard outer shells. In the mountains of the Philippines, farmers have carved rice terraces that rise up the hills like giant green steps. **White or Brown?** Brown rice and white rice come from the same plant, but they are different in important ways. Brown rice keeps its outer layer, called the bran, so it is chewier, takes longer to cook and has more fiber and vitamins. White rice has the bran removed. It cooks faster, it is softer, and it can be stored for longer, which is why many people prefer it. **One Grain, Many Dishes** There are thousands of kinds of rice. Long, thin basmati rice from India and Pakistan is the favorite in Saudi kitchens, where it becomes kabsa, mandi and many other family favorites. In India and Pakistan, cooks layer rice with spiced meat to make biryani. In Spain, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred. Japanese cooks use short, sticky rice mixed with vinegar to make sushi, while in West Africa, families share jollof rice cooked in a rich tomato and pepper sauce. Saudi Arabia even grows its own traditional red rice, called Hassawi rice, in the Al-Ahsa Oasis. **A Thirsty Crop** Rice has a problem, however: it needs a lot of water. Flooded paddies also release methane, a gas that warms the planet. To solve this, scientists are teaching farmers to flood and dry their fields in turns, which saves water and produces less methane. From a small bowl of sushi in Tokyo to a huge tray of kabsa shared by a Saudi family, this tiny grain connects billions of people every single day."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS2-VID","script":"Riyadh, Tokyo, Lagos – what do their dinner tables share? Rice! First, farmers plant young rice plants. Next, they flood the fields. After three to six months, they drain the water. Finally, they harvest. Brown rice keeps its bran, but white rice cooks faster. Both come from the same plant! Rice needs a lot of water. To solve this, farmers flood and dry their fields in turns. Description, sequence, compare and contrast, problem and solution – four structures, one article!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3","title":"Compare and contrast across cultures: \"How to Make Chicken Kabsa\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["compare and contrast rice dishes and food customs from different cultures","use evidence from a procedure and an informational article to find similarities and differences","explain what food customs tell us about the people who share them"],"objectives_ar":["مقارنة أطباق الأرز وعادات الطعام في ثقافات مختلفة.","استخدام أدلة من نص إجرائي ومقال معلوماتي لإيجاد أوجه التشابه والاختلاف.","شرح ما تخبرنا به عادات الطعام عن الناس الذين يتشاركونها."],"success_criteria":["I can list features of kabsa, biryani and paella with evidence from the texts.","I can explain two similarities and two differences using comparison words.","I can draw a conclusion about what sharing food shows about a culture."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ذكر سمات الكبسة والبرياني والباييلا بأدلة من النصين.","أستطيع شرح وجهي تشابه ووجهي اختلاف بكلمات المقارنة.","أستطيع استنتاج ما تُظهره مشاركة الطعام عن الثقافة."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"simmer","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈsɪmər/","definition":"to cook gently just below boiling","arabic":"يطهو على نار هادئة","example":"Simmer the chicken for 40 minutes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"broth","pos":"noun","ipa":"/brɔθ/","definition":"the tasty liquid in which meat or vegetables have been cooked","arabic":"مرق","example":"Add the rice to the hot broth.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"absorb","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əbˈzɔrb/","definition":"to take in liquid","arabic":"يمتص","example":"The rice absorbs the broth.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"sprinkle","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈsprɪŋkəl/","definition":"to drop small pieces over something","arabic":"يرشّ","example":"Sprinkle almonds on top.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"tray","pos":"noun","ipa":"/treɪ/","definition":"a large flat dish for carrying or serving food","arabic":"صينية","example":"Kabsa is served on a big tray.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"custom","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkʌstəm/","definition":"a way of doing something that people in a culture have followed for a long time","arabic":"عادة / عُرف","example":"Sharing food from one tray is a Saudi custom.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"hospitality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌhɑspɪˈtæləti/","definition":"being friendly and generous to guests","arabic":"كرم الضيافة","example":"Saudi hospitality is famous around the world.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"When we **compare**, we find **similarities**; when we **contrast**, we find **differences**. Comparing foods from different cultures helps us understand **how people live**, **what they value** and **what we share**.\n\nCompare dishes using the **same features** for each: **main grain**, **flavor and spices**, **cooking method**, **pan or serving dish** and **customs** (who eats it, when and how). Collect evidence from **both texts**: the kabsa procedure and the rice article.\n\nUse **comparison words**: *both, also, similarly, like, in the same way* for similarities; *but, while, however, unlike, whereas, on the other hand* for differences. Finish with a **conclusion**: *What does this show about the people?* – for example, that sharing food from one dish shows **hospitality** and **togetherness**.","examples":["Similarity: ***Both*** kabsa and biryani use long-grain basmati rice and spiced meat.","Difference: Kabsa rice is cooked ***in the broth***, ***while*** biryani is made by ***layering*** rice with spiced meat.","Difference: ***Unlike*** kabsa, paella is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred.","Similarity: Kabsa ***and*** paella are ***both*** often eaten straight from one big dish.","Difference (flavor): Kabsa gets a sour note from ***dried lime***; paella is colored and flavored with ***saffron***.","Difference (temperature): Kabsa is served hot, ***whereas*** sushi rice is served cool with vinegar.","Custom: *Kabsa is traditionally served on one big tray … family and guests share it together.*","Custom: *In India and Pakistan, many families share biryani in a similar way.*","Conclusion: ***Sharing one dish shows that in many cultures, food is about togetherness and welcoming guests.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Comparing different features (kabsa's spices vs. paella's pan). → ✓ Compare the SAME feature for each dish. (Same feature, both dishes.)","✗ Only listing differences. → ✓ Find similarities too. (Compare AND contrast.)","✗ Calling another culture's food 'strange'. → ✓ Use neutral words: different, special, traditional. (Respect.)","✗ Kabsa and biryani both uses basmati rice. → ✓ Kabsa and biryani both use basmati rice. (Plural subject → **use**.)","✗ Unlike kabsa, paella is cook in a flat pan. → ✓ Unlike kabsa, paella is cooked in a flat pan. (Passive → **cooked**.)","✗ Forgetting evidence from the texts. → ✓ Quote or paraphrase the recipe or the article. (Use evidence.)","✗ No conclusion. → ✓ Explain what the comparison shows about people. (So what?)"],"arabic":"المقارنة تعني إيجاد أوجه التشابه، والمباينة تعني إيجاد أوجه الاختلاف، ومقارنة أطعمة الثقافات المختلفة تساعدنا على فهم كيف يعيش الناس وما يقدّرونه وما نشترك فيه.\n\nقارن الأطباق بالسمات نفسها: الحبوب الأساسية، والنكهة والبهارات، وطريقة الطهي، والإناء أو طبق التقديم، والعادات.\n\nاستخدم كلمات المقارنة: both, also, similarly للتشابه، وbut, while, however, unlike, whereas للاختلاف، ثم اختم باستنتاج: ماذا يُظهر هذا عن الناس؟\n\nانتبه: استخدم كلمات محايدة ومحترمة مثل different وtraditional، لا strange."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Fry the onions, brown the chicken, simmer, add the rice… Let's make kabsa!","on_screen":"How to Make Chicken Kabsa 🍗🍚","visual":"A pot on a stove; onions turning golden."},{"time":"00:30–01:40","narration":"Stir in the spices and dried lime… pour in the water… simmer for 40 minutes…","on_screen":"Steps 1–7","visual":"Step numbers appear as each action happens; the rice absorbs the broth."},{"time":"01:40–02:40","narration":"Kabsa and biryani both use basmati rice – but biryani is layered, while kabsa cooks in the broth!","on_screen":"Both… / but… / while…","visual":"Split screen: kabsa tray, biryani pot, paella pan."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"One big tray, one wide pan, one shared pot – what does sharing food tell us about people?","on_screen":"Customs → culture","visual":"Families in three countries sharing one dish."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Compare the same features, use comparison words and draw a conclusion!","on_screen":"Same features · Comparison words · Conclusion","visual":"A comparison chart fills itself in."}],"captions_ar":["حمّر البصل، وحمّر الدجاج، واطهُ على نار هادئة، وأضف الأرز… لنُعِدّ الكبسة!","أضف البهارات واللومي… واسكب الماء… واطهُ على نار هادئة 40 دقيقة…","الكبسة والبرياني كلاهما بأرز بسمتي، لكن البرياني طبقات، أما الكبسة فتُطهى في المرق!","صينية كبيرة، ومقلاة واسعة، وقدر مشترك: ماذا تخبرنا مشاركة الطعام عن الناس؟","قارن السمات نفسها، واستخدم كلمات المقارنة، واستخلص استنتاجًا!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, cheerful male chef voice, speed 1.0."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"How to Make Chicken Kabsa","body":"**Serves:** 6 · **Time:** about 1 hour 30 minutes\n\n**Ingredients:** 1 kg chicken pieces · 3 cups basmati rice, washed and soaked for 30 minutes · 2 onions, chopped · 3 cloves of garlic, crushed · 2 tomatoes, chopped · 2 tablespoons tomato paste · 3 tablespoons oil · 1 dried lime (loomi) · 2 teaspoons kabsa spice mix (cardamom, cinnamon, cloves, cumin and black pepper) · 1 teaspoon salt · 4 cups water · a handful of fried almonds and raisins\n\n**Safety first:** Ask an adult to help with hot oil and the oven. Wash your hands and the cutting board after touching raw chicken.\n\n**1.** Heat the oil in a large pot and fry the onions until they are golden.\n\n**2.** Add the garlic and the chicken, and brown the chicken on all sides.\n\n**3.** Stir in the tomatoes, tomato paste, spices, dried lime and salt.\n\n**4.** Pour in the water and bring it to a boil. Then cover the pot and simmer for 40 minutes, until the chicken is cooked through.\n\n**5.** Take out the chicken and grill it in the oven for 10–15 minutes, until it is golden brown.\n\n**6.** Meanwhile, drain the rice and add it to the hot broth. Cover and cook on low heat for 20–25 minutes, until the rice has absorbed the liquid.\n\n**7.** Spread the rice on a large tray, place the chicken on top and sprinkle it with almonds and raisins.\n\n**Did you know?** Kabsa is traditionally served on one big tray in the middle of the table, and family and guests share it together. In India and Pakistan, many families share biryani in a similar way, and in Spain, friends often eat paella straight from its wide pan.","word_count":279,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS3-1","genre":"procedural","role":"paired","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[{"word":"simmer","definition":"to cook gently just below boiling","arabic":"يطهو على نار هادئة"},{"word":"broth","definition":"the tasty liquid in which meat has been cooked","arabic":"مرق"},{"word":"loomi","definition":"a dried lime used in Gulf cooking","arabic":"لومي (ليمون أسود مجفف)"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the recipe. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ الوصفة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"How long should you soak the basmati rice for kabsa?","answer":"30 minutes","explanation":"Ingredients.","explanation_ar":"30 دقيقة.","options":["30 minutes","40 minutes","10 minutes","2 hours"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What should you do FIRST in the kabsa recipe?","answer":"Heat the oil and fry the onions until golden.","explanation":"Step 1.","explanation_ar":"تسخين الزيت وقلي البصل.","options":["Add the rice to the broth.","Heat the oil and fry the onions until golden.","Grill the chicken.","Sprinkle almonds and raisins."],"dok":1,"skill":"sequence","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long does the chicken simmer?","answer":"40 minutes","explanation":"Step 4.","explanation_ar":"40 دقيقة.","options":["40 minutes","15 minutes","25 minutes","90 minutes"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What gives kabsa its sour flavor, according to the ingredients?","answer":"one dried lime (loomi)","explanation":"Ingredients.","explanation_ar":"اللومي.","options":["raisins","tomato paste","one dried lime (loomi)","almonds"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What should you do after touching raw chicken?","answer":"Wash your hands and the cutting board.","explanation":"Safety first.","explanation_ar":"اغسل يديك ولوح التقطيع.","options":["Add more salt.","Turn off the oven.","Wash your hands and the cutting board.","Taste the rice."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does *Meanwhile* in Step 6 tell you?","answer":"You cook the rice at the same time as the chicken is grilling.","explanation":"Time signal.","explanation_ar":"في أثناء ذلك.","options":["You wait until the next day.","You stop cooking.","You start again.","You cook the rice at the same time as the chicken is grilling."],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Compare and contrast across cultures. Choose.","instructions_ar":"قارن بين الثقافات: اختر.","items":[{"q":"How are kabsa and biryani SIMILAR?","answer":"Both use basmati rice and spiced meat.","explanation":"Similarity.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما بأرز بسمتي ولحم متبّل.","options":["Both are cooked in a flat pan.","Both use saffron and seafood.","Both use basmati rice and spiced meat.","Both are served cold."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How is paella DIFFERENT from kabsa?","answer":"It is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred.","explanation":"Difference.","explanation_ar":"مقلاة مسطحة وزعفران.","options":["It uses rice.","It is cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred.","It is shared from one dish.","It has meat."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a CONTRAST word correctly?","answer":"Kabsa rice cooks in the broth, while biryani is made in layers.","explanation":"Contrast word.","explanation_ar":"while.","options":["Kabsa rice cooks in the broth, while biryani is made in layers.","Kabsa rice cooks in the broth, both biryani is made in layers.","Kabsa rice cooks in the broth, also biryani is made in layers.","Kabsa rice cooks in the broth, similarly biryani is made in layers."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparison words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which custom do kabsa, biryani and paella share, according to the texts?","answer":"They are often eaten together from one big dish.","explanation":"Evidence from both texts.","explanation_ar":"تؤكل معًا من طبق واحد.","options":["They are eaten only at breakfast.","They are always eaten alone.","They are eaten with chopsticks.","They are often eaten together from one big dish."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which feature should you compare for EACH dish to make a fair comparison?","answer":"the cooking method","explanation":"Same feature.","explanation_ar":"طريقة الطهي.","options":["the price in one shop","the color of one plate","the cooking method","the name of the cook"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does sharing food from one tray MOST likely show about a culture?","answer":"People value togetherness and welcoming guests.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"قيمة الترابط والضيافة.","options":["People don't have enough plates.","People don't like cooking.","People eat very quickly.","People value togetherness and welcoming guests."],"dok":3,"skill":"drawing conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Cross-Culture Comparison Chart.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جدول المقارنة بين الثقافات.","items":[{"q":"Write the main features of kabsa from the recipe.","answer":"basmati rice; spices and dried lime; rice cooked in the chicken broth; served on one big tray and shared","explanation":"Kabsa.","explanation_ar":"الكبسة.","dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write the main features of biryani and paella from the rice article.","answer":"Biryani: rice layered with spiced meat (India and Pakistan). Paella: cooked in a wide, flat pan with saffron, and the rice is not stirred (Spain).","explanation":"Other dishes.","explanation_ar":"الأطباق الأخرى.","dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write two similarities and two differences using comparison words.","answer":"Similarities: All three use rice and meat, and they are all often shared from one dish. Differences: Kabsa rice cooks in broth, while biryani is layered; unlike kabsa, paella uses saffron and a flat pan.","explanation":"Compare/contrast.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Write a conclusion about what the comparison shows.","answer":"Even though the dishes are cooked in different ways, they show that people in many cultures use food to bring family and guests together.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الاستنتاج.","dok":3,"skill":"drawing conclusions","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Compare, connect and create.","instructions_ar":"قارن واربط وأنشئ.","items":[{"q":"How is the purpose of the kabsa text different from the purpose of the rice article?","answer":"The kabsa text teaches readers how to cook one dish step by step, while the rice article informs readers about rice around the world.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الغرض.","dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose","stage":"production"},{"q":"Find two collocations from LS1 in the recipe and explain what they mean.","answer":"fry the onions – cook them in hot oil; bring it to a boil – heat it until it bubbles strongly.","explanation":"Collocations.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمات.","dok":2,"skill":"collocations","stage":"production"},{"q":"Compare kabsa with a dish your family eats at Eid or on special days.","answer":"Like kabsa, our Eid dish of jareesh is shared by the whole family, but it is made from crushed wheat instead of rice.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":3,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one welcoming sentence a Saudi host might say when serving kabsa to guests from another country.","answer":"Welcome! Please sit with us – in our home, we always share our food together.","explanation":"Culture.","explanation_ar":"الثقافة.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"How to Make Chicken Kabsa\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “How to Make Chicken Kabsa”.","answer":"279 words","title":"How to Make Chicken Kabsa","body":"**Serves:** 6 · **Time:** about 1 hour 30 minutes\n\n**Ingredients:** 1 kg chicken pieces · 3 cups basmati rice, washed and soaked for 30 minutes · 2 onions, chopped · 3 cloves of garlic, crushed · 2 tomatoes, chopped · 2 tablespoons tomato paste · 3 tablespoons oil · 1 dried lime (loomi) · 2 teaspoons kabsa spice mix (cardamom, cinnamon, cloves, cumin and black pepper) · 1 teaspoon salt · 4 cups water · a handful of fried almonds and raisins\n\n**Safety first:** Ask an adult to help with hot oil and the oven. Wash your hands and the cutting board after touching raw chicken.\n\n**1.** Heat the oil in a large pot and fry the onions until they are golden.\n\n**2.** Add the garlic and the chicken, and brown the chicken on all sides.\n\n**3.** Stir in the tomatoes, tomato paste, spices, dried lime and salt.\n\n**4.** Pour in the water and bring it to a boil. Then cover the pot and simmer for 40 minutes, until the chicken is cooked through.\n\n**5.** Take out the chicken and grill it in the oven for 10–15 minutes, until it is golden brown.\n\n**6.** Meanwhile, drain the rice and add it to the hot broth. Cover and cook on low heat for 20–25 minutes, until the rice has absorbed the liquid.\n\n**7.** Spread the rice on a large tray, place the chicken on top and sprinkle it with almonds and raisins.\n\n**Did you know?** Kabsa is traditionally served on one big tray in the middle of the table, and family and guests share it together. In India and Pakistan, many families share biryani in a similar way, and in Spain, friends often eat paella straight from its wide pan.","words":279,"limit":160}],"time_limit_sec":160},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Cross-Culture Comparison Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Use both texts. Write the features of each dish, then two similarities, two differences and a conclusion.","items":[{"q":"Kabsa – grain, flavor, cooking method, serving","answer":"basmati rice; spices + dried lime; rice cooked in the chicken broth; shared from one big tray"},{"q":"Biryani – grain, cooking method, serving","answer":"basmati rice; rice layered with spiced meat; often shared by the family"},{"q":"Paella – grain, flavor, pan, cooking method","answer":"rice; saffron; wide, flat pan; rice is not stirred; friends eat from the pan"},{"q":"Two similarities","answer":"All use rice with meat and spices; all are often shared from one dish."},{"q":"Two differences","answer":"Kabsa rice cooks in broth, while biryani is layered; paella uses saffron and a flat pan, unlike kabsa."},{"q":"Conclusion: what sharing food shows about people","answer":"In many cultures, sharing one dish shows hospitality, togetherness and welcoming guests."}]}],"game":{"name":"Dish Detectives Around the World","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Mystery dish: the app gives comparison clues (e.g., 'Like kabsa, it uses rice, but unlike kabsa, it is cooked in a flat pan with saffron'); the child guesses the dish and pins it on the map.","A team chooses a secret dish card.","They give three comparison clues using both, like, while or unlike.","Other teams guess the dish.","The guessing team must name one similarity and one difference with kabsa.","Correct guess = 2 points; good clue sentences = 1 point each."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does it mean to *contrast* two things?","answer":"to find the differences between them","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"إيجاد الاختلافات.","options":["to find only the similarities","to cook them together","to find the differences between them","to describe one of them"],"dok":1,"skill":"compare and contrast"},{"q":"What does *simmer* mean?","answer":"to cook gently just below boiling","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"يطهو على نار هادئة.","options":["to fry in a lot of oil","to cook gently just below boiling","to freeze","to bake at a very high heat"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Cook until the rice has **absorbed** the liquid.* *Absorbed* means ___.","answer":"taken in","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"امتصّ.","options":["spilled","taken in","boiled away","frozen"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word shows a SIMILARITY?","answer":"both","explanation":"Comparison word.","explanation_ar":"both.","options":["both","while","unlike","however"],"dok":1,"skill":"comparison words"},{"q":"Which sentence about two rice dishes is correct?","answer":"Kabsa and biryani both use basmati rice.","explanation":"Plural → use.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Kabsa and biryani both uses basmati rice.","Kabsa and biryani both using basmati rice.","Kabsa and biryani is both use basmati rice.","Kabsa and biryani both use basmati rice."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which is a DIFFERENCE between kabsa and sushi?","answer":"Kabsa is served hot, whereas sushi rice is served cool with vinegar.","explanation":"Difference.","explanation_ar":"اختلاف.","options":["Both use rice.","Both are eaten in many countries.","Kabsa is served hot, whereas sushi rice is served cool with vinegar.","Both are popular dishes."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast"},{"q":"What is *hospitality*?","answer":"being friendly and generous to guests","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"كرم الضيافة.","options":["a hospital kitchen","being friendly and generous to guests","a kind of spice","a cooking method"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Why should you compare the SAME features for each dish?","answer":"so the comparison is fair and clear","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لتكون المقارنة عادلة.","options":["so the text is longer","so you can skip evidence","so you only find differences","so the comparison is fair and clear"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast"},{"q":"Which conclusion is BEST supported by both texts?","answer":"Many cultures use shared rice dishes to bring people together.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج مدعوم.","options":["Many cultures use shared rice dishes to bring people together.","Paella is better than kabsa.","Only Saudi families share food.","Rice is cooked the same way everywhere."],"dok":3,"skill":"drawing conclusions"},{"q":"Which word is the most respectful way to describe a dish from another culture that is new to you?","answer":"different","explanation":"Respect.","explanation_ar":"مختلف.","options":["different","weird","strange","bad"],"dok":3,"skill":"cultural awareness"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'simmer': a pot with small bubbles on low heat"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'broth': a pot of golden liquid"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'absorb': rice grains swelling with liquid"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sprinkle': a hand dropping almonds over rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tray': a large round tray of rice and chicken"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'custom': a family eating together from one tray"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hospitality': a host pouring coffee for guests"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-01","prompt":"A family sharing chicken kabsa from one tray."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-02","prompt":"The seven steps for making kabsa."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS3-03","prompt":"Families in three cultures sharing rice dishes."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V01","script":"simmer. Simmer the chicken for 40 minutes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V02","script":"broth. Add the rice to the hot broth."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V03","script":"absorb. The rice absorbs the broth."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V04","script":"sprinkle. Sprinkle almonds on top."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V05","script":"tray. Kabsa is served on a big tray."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V06","script":"custom. Sharing food from one tray is a Saudi custom."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-V07","script":"hospitality. Saudi hospitality is famous around the world."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-T1","script":"**Serves:** 6 · **Time:** about 1 hour 30 minutes **Ingredients:** 1 kg chicken pieces · 3 cups basmati rice, washed and soaked for 30 minutes · 2 onions, chopped · 3 cloves of garlic, crushed · 2 tomatoes, chopped · 2 tablespoons tomato paste · 3 tablespoons oil · 1 dried lime (loomi) · 2 teaspoons kabsa spice mix (cardamom, cinnamon, cloves, cumin and black pepper) · 1 teaspoon salt · 4 cups water · a handful of fried almonds and raisins **Safety first:** Ask an adult to help with hot oil and the oven. Wash your hands and the cutting board after touching raw chicken. **1.** Heat the oil in a large pot and fry the onions until they are golden. **2.** Add the garlic and the chicken, and brown the chicken on all sides. **3.** Stir in the tomatoes, tomato paste, spices, dried lime and salt. **4.** Pour in the water and bring it to a boil. Then cover the pot and simmer for 40 minutes, until the chicken is cooked through. **5.** Take out the chicken and grill it in the oven for 10–15 minutes, until it is golden brown. **6.** Meanwhile, drain the rice and add it to the hot broth. Cover and cook on low heat for 20–25 minutes, until the rice has absorbed the liquid. **7.** Spread the rice on a large tray, place the chicken on top and sprinkle it with almonds and raisins. **Did you know?** Kabsa is traditionally served on one big tray in the middle of the table, and family and guests share it together. In India and Pakistan, many families share biryani in a similar way, and in Spain, friends often eat paella straight from its wide pan."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS3-VID","script":"Fry the onions, brown the chicken, simmer, add the rice… Let's make kabsa! Stir in the spices and dried lime… pour in the water… simmer for 40 minutes… Kabsa and biryani both use basmati rice – but biryani is layered, while kabsa cooks in the broth! One big tray, one wide pan, one shared pot – what does sharing food tell us about people? Compare the same features, use comparison words and draw a conclusion!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Block and point-by-point comparison","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["explain the block method and the point-by-point method of organizing a comparison","plan a comparison with the same features in the same order","write comparison sentences with a topic sentence, transitions and comparison words"],"objectives_ar":["شرح طريقة الكتلة وطريقة النقطة بنقطة في تنظيم المقارنة.","التخطيط لمقارنة بالسمات نفسها وبالترتيب نفسه.","كتابة جمل مقارنة بجملة موضوع وكلمات انتقال وكلمات مقارنة."],"success_criteria":["I can tell whether a paragraph uses the block or the point-by-point method.","I can plan both methods for the same two items with the same features.","I can write a point-by-point paragraph with a topic sentence and comparison words."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تمييز الفقرة المكتوبة بطريقة الكتلة أو النقطة بنقطة.","أستطيع التخطيط للطريقتين للعنصرين نفسيهما بالسمات نفسها.","أستطيع كتابة فقرة بطريقة النقطة بنقطة بجملة موضوع وكلمات مقارنة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"block method","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈblɑk ˌmɛθəd/","definition":"a way of comparing that describes all of item A, then all of item B","arabic":"طريقة الكتلة","example":"The block method describes one bread, then the other.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"point-by-point","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌpɔɪnt baɪ ˈpɔɪnt/","definition":"comparing two items one feature at a time","arabic":"نقطة بنقطة","example":"The point-by-point method compares shape, then baking.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"flatbread","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈflætˌbrɛd/","definition":"a thin, flat bread","arabic":"خبز مسطّح","example":"Tamees is a large, round flatbread.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"loaf","pos":"noun","ipa":"/loʊf/","definition":"bread baked in one piece (plural: loaves)","arabic":"رغيف","example":"A baguette is a long, thin loaf.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"crust","pos":"noun","ipa":"/krʌst/","definition":"the hard outer part of bread","arabic":"قشرة الخبز","example":"The crust is golden and crunchy.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"whereas","pos":"conjunction","ipa":"/wɛrˈæz/","definition":"a word that shows a contrast between two facts","arabic":"بينما","example":"Tamees is round, whereas a baguette is long.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"topic sentence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɑpɪk ˌsɛntəns/","definition":"the sentence that tells what a paragraph is about","arabic":"الجملة الرئيسة (جملة الموضوع)","example":"The topic sentence names both breads.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"There are two main ways to organize a comparison. In the **block method**, you write **everything about item A first**, then **everything about item B** – using the **same features in the same order**. In the **point-by-point method**, you compare **both items one feature at a time**: shape (A and B), then baking (A and B), then eating (A and B).\n\n**Block** works well for **short** comparisons and when each item needs some description. **Point-by-point** makes **similarities and differences very clear**, because the reader sees both items side by side. Both methods need a **topic sentence** that names the two items and (usually) the features.\n\nUseful language: **transitions** – *First, Second, Finally, On the other hand, In the same way*; **comparison words** – *both, also, like, similarly* (similar) and *but, while, whereas, unlike, however* (different). Always **plan** with a chart before you write.","examples":["Topic sentence: ***Tamees and the baguette are both popular breads, but they differ in shape, baking method and the way people eat them.***","Block – A: ***Tamees is a large, round flatbread … baked on the hot wall of a clay oven … eaten for breakfast with beans, cheese or honey.***","Block – transition to B: ***A baguette, on the other hand, is a long, thin loaf from France.***","Point 1 (shape): ***First, tamees is large, round and flat, whereas a baguette is long and thin.***","Point 2 (baking): ***Second, tamees is baked on the hot wall of a clay oven, while a baguette is baked inside an ordinary hot oven.***","Point 3 (eating): ***Finally, Saudis usually eat tamees with beans or honey, but the French often eat baguettes with butter, jam or soup.***","Similarity: ***Both*** breads are made from wheat flour and are best when fresh.","Using *unlike*: ***Unlike*** a baguette, tamees is baked on the wall of a clay oven.","Concluding sentence: ***Although they look very different, both breads are a delicious part of everyday life in their countries.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Block: tamees's shape and baking, then the baguette's price and color. → ✓ Use the SAME features in the SAME order for both. (Same features, same order.)","✗ Mixing the methods in one paragraph by accident. → ✓ Plan first: block OR point-by-point. (Choose one method.)","✗ No topic sentence. → ✓ Start with a sentence that names both items. (Name both.)","✗ Tamees is round, whereas the baguette long. → ✓ Tamees is round, whereas the baguette is long. (Each part needs a verb.)","✗ Both breads is popular. → ✓ Both breads are popular. (both + plural verb.)","✗ Unlike tamees, a baguette baked in an oven. → ✓ Unlike tamees, a baguette is baked in an oven. (Passive: **is baked**.)","✗ Only differences. → ✓ Add at least one similarity. (Compare AND contrast.)"],"arabic":"هناك طريقتان لتنظيم المقارنة: طريقة الكتلة، وفيها تكتب كل شيء عن العنصر الأول ثم كل شيء عن الثاني بالسمات نفسها وبالترتيب نفسه، وطريقة النقطة بنقطة، وفيها تقارن العنصرين سمةً سمة.\n\nطريقة الكتلة مناسبة للمقارنات القصيرة، وطريقة النقطة بنقطة توضّح أوجه التشابه والاختلاف جيدًا لأن القارئ يرى العنصرين جنبًا إلى جنب، وتحتاج الطريقتان إلى جملة موضوع تذكر العنصرين.\n\nاستخدم كلمات الانتقال (First, Second, Finally, On the other hand) وكلمات المقارنة (both, also, like للتشابه، وbut, while, whereas, unlike للاختلاف)، وخطّط بجدول قبل الكتابة.\n\nانتبه: both يتبعها فعل جمع، وكل جزء بعد whereas يحتاج إلى فعل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"One bread from Saudi Arabia, one from France – and two smart ways to compare them!","on_screen":"Block vs. Point-by-Point 🥖🫓","visual":"A round tamees and a long baguette on a table."},{"time":"00:30–01:30","narration":"Tamees is a large, round flatbread… A baguette, on the other hand, is a long, thin loaf…","on_screen":"Block method: A → B","visual":"Two big blocks stack: all tamees, then all baguette."},{"time":"01:30–02:40","narration":"First, shape… Second, baking… Finally, eating – both breads, one point at a time!","on_screen":"Point-by-point: A+B, A+B, A+B","visual":"A zipper closes, joining tamees and baguette features."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Both, while, whereas, unlike, on the other hand – your comparison toolkit!","on_screen":"Comparison words","visual":"Words light up between the two breads."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Same features, same order, a clear topic sentence – then choose your method!","on_screen":"Plan → choose → write","visual":"A planning chart turns into a paragraph."}],"captions_ar":["خبز من السعودية وآخر من فرنسا، وطريقتان ذكيتان للمقارنة بينهما!","التميس خبز مسطح دائري كبير… أما الباغيت فرغيف طويل رفيع…","أولًا الشكل… ثانيًا الخَبز… وأخيرًا طريقة الأكل: الخبزان معًا نقطةً نقطة!","both وwhile وwhereas وunlike وon the other hand: أدوات المقارنة!","السمات نفسها، والترتيب نفسه، وجملة موضوع واضحة، ثم اختر طريقتك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female voice, speed 1.0. Lama reads both paragraphs."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Two Breads, Two Ways to Compare: Tamees and Baguette (by Lama)","body":"**Block method:** Tamees is a large, round flatbread that is popular across Saudi Arabia. It is baked on the hot wall of a clay oven, so it is crispy outside and soft inside. People usually eat it for breakfast with beans, cheese or honey. A baguette, on the other hand, is a long, thin loaf from France. It is baked in a hot oven until its crust is golden and crunchy. French families often buy fresh baguettes every day and eat them with butter, jam or soup.\n\n**Point-by-point method:** Tamees and the baguette are both popular breads, but they differ in shape, baking method and the way people eat them. First, tamees is large, round and flat, whereas a baguette is long and thin. Second, tamees is baked on the hot wall of a clay oven, while a baguette is baked inside an ordinary hot oven. Finally, Saudis usually eat tamees with beans or honey for breakfast, but the French often eat baguettes with butter, jam or soup.","word_count":168,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Block method:** everything about **tamees** first (shape → baking → eating), then everything about the **baguette** in the **same order**.","**Transition between blocks:** *A baguette, on the other hand, …* tells the reader that the second item begins.","**Point-by-point method:** a **topic sentence** names both breads and the **three features** (*shape, baking method, the way people eat them*).","**One feature at a time:** each sentence compares both breads on **one feature**, with *First, Second, Finally*.","**Comparison words:** *both, whereas, while, but* show similarity and difference inside each sentence.","**Same facts, different organization:** the facts are the same; only the order changes."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Lama's paragraphs. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ فقرتي لمى واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where is tamees baked, according to Lama?","answer":"on the hot wall of a clay oven","explanation":"Both paragraphs.","explanation_ar":"على جدار فرن طيني.","options":["on the hot wall of a clay oven","in a frying pan","on a grill outside","in a microwave"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is a baguette, according to Lama?","answer":"a long, thin loaf from France","explanation":"Block paragraph.","explanation_ar":"رغيف فرنسي طويل.","options":["a round flatbread from Saudi Arabia","a kind of cake","a long, thin loaf from France","a rice dish"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do people usually eat with tamees for breakfast?","answer":"beans, cheese or honey","explanation":"Key detail.","explanation_ar":"فول أو جبن أو عسل.","options":["butter, jam or soup","beans, cheese or honey","rice and chicken","fish"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the BLOCK paragraph, what does Lama describe first?","answer":"everything about tamees","explanation":"Block method.","explanation_ar":"كل ما يتعلق بالتميس.","options":["the shape of both breads","everything about the baguette","the price of both breads","everything about tamees"],"dok":1,"skill":"block method","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which three features does Lama compare?","answer":"shape, baking method and the way people eat them","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"الشكل والخبز وطريقة الأكل.","options":["shape, baking method and the way people eat them","color, price and size","taste, smell and weight","history, name and shop"],"dok":1,"skill":"point-by-point","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which phrase moves from tamees to the baguette in the block paragraph?","answer":"on the other hand","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"on the other hand.","options":["First,","Finally,","on the other hand","whereas"],"dok":2,"skill":"transitions","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Block or point-by-point? Choose.","instructions_ar":"الكتلة أم النقطة بنقطة؟ اختر.","items":[{"q":"*Laban is a cold yogurt drink, whereas Saudi coffee is served hot.* Which method does this sentence show?","answer":"point-by-point","explanation":"Both items, one feature.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","options":["block","neither","point-by-point","a recipe"],"dok":2,"skill":"point-by-point","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In a BLOCK comparison of dates and chocolate, what must stay the same?","answer":"the features and their order for both items","explanation":"Same features, same order.","explanation_ar":"السمات وترتيبها.","options":["the number of words in each sentence","the features and their order for both items","the colors of the two foods","the first letter of each sentence"],"dok":2,"skill":"block method","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for a point-by-point comparison of falafel and burgers?","answer":"Falafel and burgers are both popular street foods, but they differ in ingredients, cooking method and origin.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة موضوع.","options":["Falafel is tasty.","I like burgers more.","Burgers are from many places.","Falafel and burgers are both popular street foods, but they differ in ingredients, cooking method and origin."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence comparing two breads is grammatically correct?","answer":"Both breads are made from wheat flour.","explanation":"both + plural.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Both breads are made from wheat flour.","Both breads is made from wheat flour.","Both bread are made from wheat flour.","Both breads making from wheat flour."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"When is the POINT-BY-POINT method especially useful?","answer":"when you want the reader to see similarities and differences clearly, side by side","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"لإبراز أوجه التشابه والاختلاف.","options":["when you only describe one item","when you want the reader to see similarities and differences clearly, side by side","when you write a story","when you have no features to compare"],"dok":2,"skill":"point-by-point","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A student's block paragraph describes tamees's shape and baking, then the baguette's price and color. What is wrong?","answer":"The features are not the same for both breads.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"السمات غير متطابقة.","options":["It has too many breads.","It uses comparison words.","It has a topic sentence.","The features are not the same for both breads."],"dok":3,"skill":"block method","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the comparison with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل المقارنة من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Kabsa and mandi are ________ rice dishes with meat.","answer":"both","explanation":"Similarity.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Kabsa is cooked in a pot, ________ mandi is cooked in an underground oven.","answer":"whereas","explanation":"Contrast.","explanation_ar":"بينما.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, kabsa uses many spices.","answer":"First","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"أولًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Mandi, on the other ________, has a smoky flavor.","answer":"hand","explanation":"Transition.","explanation_ar":"من ناحية أخرى.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________ kabsa, mandi is cooked with the meat hanging above the rice.","answer":"Unlike","explanation":"Contrast.","explanation_ar":"على عكس.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Kabsa is popular in Saudi Arabia, and mandi is ________ popular there.","answer":"also","explanation":"Similarity.","explanation_ar":"أيضًا.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Plan and write comparisons.","instructions_ar":"خطّط للمقارنات واكتبها.","items":[{"q":"Plan a comparison of laban and orange juice: list three features and a fact for each drink.","answer":"Taste: laban is salty and sour; orange juice is sweet. Temperature: both are served cold. Made from: laban from milk; orange juice from fruit.","explanation":"Plan.","explanation_ar":"خطّط.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a topic sentence for your laban and orange juice comparison.","answer":"Laban and orange juice are both cold drinks, but they differ in taste and in what they are made from.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"جملة الموضوع.","dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write the comparison in the point-by-point method (3 sentences).","answer":"First, laban is salty and a little sour, whereas orange juice is sweet. Second, both drinks are usually served cold. Finally, laban is made from milk, while orange juice is made from fruit.","explanation":"Point-by-point.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","dok":3,"skill":"point-by-point","stage":"production"},{"q":"Rewrite your comparison in the block method.","answer":"Laban is a salty, slightly sour drink made from milk, and it is usually served cold. Orange juice, on the other hand, is a sweet drink made from fruit, and it is also served cold.","explanation":"Block.","explanation_ar":"الكتلة.","dok":3,"skill":"block method","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Zip It or Stack It!","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Paragraph builder: the child receives sentence blocks and chooses STACK (block) or ZIP (point-by-point); in ZIP mode, the child must alternate A and B sentences feature by feature. A correct paragraph serves a virtual meal.","Each team receives sentence strips for one pair of foods.","The teacher calls 'Stack it!' or 'Zip it!'.","Teams arrange the strips into a block or a point-by-point paragraph.","They add one comparison word to each sentence where possible.","Correct order = 3 points; correct comparison words = 1 point each."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is the *block method* of comparison?","answer":"describing all of item A, then all of item B","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"طريقة الكتلة.","options":["comparing both items one feature at a time","describing only one item","listing ingredients","describing all of item A, then all of item B"],"dok":1,"skill":"block method"},{"q":"What is a *loaf*?","answer":"bread baked in one piece","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"رغيف.","options":["bread baked in one piece","a slice of cheese","a cooking pot","a kind of rice"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Tamees is round, **whereas** a baguette is long.* *Whereas* shows ___.","answer":"a contrast","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"تضاد.","options":["a contrast","a similarity","a time order","a reason"],"dok":1,"skill":"comparison words"},{"q":"Which word in a bread comparison shows a SIMILARITY?","answer":"similarly","explanation":"Comparison word.","explanation_ar":"similarly.","options":["whereas","unlike","similarly","but"],"dok":1,"skill":"comparison words"},{"q":"Which sentence comparing two dishes is correct?","answer":"Unlike kabsa, mandi is cooked in an underground oven.","explanation":"Passive.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Unlike kabsa, mandi cooked in an underground oven.","Unlike kabsa, mandi is cook in an underground oven.","Unlike kabsa, mandi is cooked in an underground oven.","Unlike kabsa, mandi are cooked in an underground oven."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What should a comparison topic sentence include?","answer":"the two items and, usually, the features you will compare","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"العنصران والسمات.","options":["only your favorite item","a recipe","a question about the weather","the two items and, usually, the features you will compare"],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences"},{"q":"Which sentence is written in the POINT-BY-POINT style?","answer":"Dates grow on palm trees, while cocoa beans for chocolate grow on cacao trees.","explanation":"Point-by-point.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","options":["Dates are sweet fruits from palm trees.","Dates grow on palm trees, while cocoa beans for chocolate grow on cacao trees.","Chocolate is made from cocoa beans.","I love both dates and chocolate."],"dok":2,"skill":"point-by-point"},{"q":"Why must the block method use the same features in the same order?","answer":"so the reader can easily compare the two items","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"ليسهل على القارئ المقارنة.","options":["so the reader can easily compare the two items","so the paragraph is longer","so you don't need a topic sentence","so the items look the same"],"dok":2,"skill":"block method"},{"q":"A comparison paragraph lists only differences. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Add at least one similarity with a word like both or also.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف وجه تشابه.","options":["Remove the topic sentence.","Add at least one similarity with a word like both or also.","Add more differences.","Change it into a story."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"For a long comparison of two dishes with many details, which method usually makes the differences EASIEST to see?","answer":"point-by-point","explanation":"Choosing a method.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","options":["block","a list of ingredients only","point-by-point","a single sentence"],"dok":3,"skill":"choosing a method"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'block method': two separate boxes, A then B"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'point-by-point': a zipper joining A and B"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'flatbread': a round flat loaf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'loaf': a long French loaf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'crust': a close-up of a bread crust"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'whereas': a balance scale with two different items"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'topic sentence': the first line of a paragraph highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-01","prompt":"Tamees and a baguette side by side."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-02","prompt":"Block and point-by-point methods shown as stacking and zipping."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS4-03","prompt":"A comparison planning chart."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V01","script":"block method. The block method describes one bread, then the other."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V02","script":"point-by-point. The point-by-point method compares shape, then baking."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V03","script":"flatbread. Tamees is a large, round flatbread."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V04","script":"loaf. A baguette is a long, thin loaf."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V05","script":"crust. The crust is golden and crunchy."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V06","script":"whereas. Tamees is round, whereas a baguette is long."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-V07","script":"topic sentence. The topic sentence names both breads."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-T1","script":"**Block method:** Tamees is a large, round flatbread that is popular across Saudi Arabia. It is baked on the hot wall of a clay oven, so it is crispy outside and soft inside. People usually eat it for breakfast with beans, cheese or honey. A baguette, on the other hand, is a long, thin loaf from France. It is baked in a hot oven until its crust is golden and crunchy. French families often buy fresh baguettes every day and eat them with butter, jam or soup. **Point-by-point method:** Tamees and the baguette are both popular breads, but they differ in shape, baking method and the way people eat them. First, tamees is large, round and flat, whereas a baguette is long and thin. Second, tamees is baked on the hot wall of a clay oven, while a baguette is baked inside an ordinary hot oven. Finally, Saudis usually eat tamees with beans or honey for breakfast, but the French often eat baguettes with butter, jam or soup."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS4-VID","script":"One bread from Saudi Arabia, one from France – and two smart ways to compare them! Tamees is a large, round flatbread… A baguette, on the other hand, is a long, thin loaf… First, shape… Second, baking… Finally, eating – both breads, one point at a time! Both, while, whereas, unlike, on the other hand – your comparison toolkit! Same features, same order, a clear topic sentence – then choose your method!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A three-paragraph comparison of two traditional dishes","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a three-paragraph comparison of two traditional dishes with the Comparison Planner","write 150–200 words using the block or point-by-point method, comparison words and evidence from the fact files","revise, edit and publish the comparison in a class 'World Food Festival' menu book"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لمقارنة من ثلاث فقرات بين طبقين تقليديين بمخطط المقارنة.","كتابة 150–200 كلمة بطريقة الكتلة أو النقطة بنقطة مع كلمات المقارنة وأدلة من ملفات الحقائق.","تحسين المقارنة وتدقيقها ونشرها في كتيّب «مهرجان الطعام العالمي» الصفي."],"success_criteria":["My introduction has a hook and a topic sentence naming both dishes and the features.","My body compares the same features for both dishes with comparison words and facts.","My conclusion gives the main similarity and what the dishes show about people (150–200 words)."],"success_criteria_ar":["مقدمتي فيها جملة جذب وجملة موضوع تذكر الطبقين والسمات.","المتن يقارن السمات نفسها للطبقين بكلمات المقارنة والحقائق.","خاتمتي تذكر أهم وجه تشابه وما يُظهره الطبقان عن الناس (150–200 كلمة)."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"region","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈridʒən/","definition":"a large area of a country or the world","arabic":"منطقة / إقليم","example":"Mandi comes from the Hadramawt region of Yemen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"tender","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈtɛndər/","definition":"soft and easy to cut or chew","arabic":"طريّ","example":"The mandi meat is very tender.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"smoky","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈsmoʊki/","definition":"tasting or smelling of smoke","arabic":"مُدخَّن / بنكهة الدخان","example":"Mandi has a smoky flavor.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"fragrant","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈfreɪɡrənt/","definition":"having a sweet or pleasant smell","arabic":"عطِر / فوّاح","example":"Biryani is rich and fragrant.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"savory","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈseɪvəri/","definition":"salty or spicy, not sweet","arabic":"مالح / حادّ (غير حلو)","example":"Paella is a savory dish.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"gathering","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɡæðərɪŋ/","definition":"a meeting of people, often family or friends","arabic":"تجمّع / لمّة","example":"Mandi is served at big gatherings.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"menu","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛnju/","definition":"a list of the food you can choose in a restaurant","arabic":"قائمة الطعام","example":"Our class made a World Food Festival menu.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **comparison essay** explains how two things are **alike** and **different**. **Paragraph 1 – Introduction:** a **hook** (a question, a picture in words or a surprising fact) + a **topic sentence** that names **both dishes** and the **features** you will compare.\n\n**Paragraph 2 – Body:** compare the **same features** for both dishes – for example, **origin**, **cooking method** and **flavor** – using the **point-by-point** method (or the **block** method). Use **facts** from the fact files and **comparison words**: *both, also, similarly / whereas, while, unlike, on the other hand*.\n\n**Paragraph 3 – Conclusion:** state the **main similarity**, explain **what the dishes show about the people** who eat them and end with a **final thought** (an invitation, a wish or your preference). Keep it **150–200 words** and use the **present simple** for facts.","examples":["Hook: ***Imagine two huge trays of rice and meat at a family gathering – one spicy and golden, the other smoky and tender.***","Topic sentence: ***Kabsa and mandi are two of the most loved rice dishes in Saudi Arabia, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor.***","Origin: ***Kabsa comes from Saudi Arabia, whereas mandi comes from the Hadramawt region of Yemen.***","Cooking method: ***Kabsa is cooked in one pot, while the meat for mandi hangs above the rice in an oven dug into the ground.***","Flavor: ***As a result, kabsa tastes warm and spicy, but mandi has a smoky flavor.***","Similarity: ***Both dishes, however, are served on large trays and shared with family and guests.***","Main similarity: ***In the end, both dishes are more than food: they bring people together.***","What it shows: ***They show how much Saudis value generosity and sharing.***","Final thought: ***If you ever visit, you should try both – and decide which one you like best!***"],"common_errors":["✗ Comparing different features for each dish. → ✓ Use the same features: origin, method, flavor. (Same features.)","✗ Copying the fact file notes. → ✓ Turn notes into full sentences in your own words. (Paraphrase.)","✗ Mandi come from Yemen. → ✓ Mandi comes from Yemen. (Singular → **comes**.)","✗ Mandi is cooked in a ground oven, kabsa in one pot. → ✓ Mandi is cooked in an oven dug into the ground, while kabsa is cooked in one pot. (Use a comparison word.)","✗ Calling one dish 'better' with no reason. → ✓ If you give a preference, explain why. (Support opinions.)","✗ No conclusion. → ✓ End with the main similarity and a final thought. (Close the essay.)","✗ Writing 250 words. → ✓ Keep it 150–200 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"مقال المقارنة يشرح أوجه التشابه والاختلاف بين شيئين. المقدمة: جملة جذب وجملة موضوع تذكر الطبقين والسمات التي ستقارن بينها.\n\nالمتن: قارن السمات نفسها للطبقين (الأصل وطريقة الطهي والنكهة) بطريقة النقطة بنقطة أو الكتلة، مستخدمًا حقائق من الملفات وكلمات المقارنة.\n\nالخاتمة: اذكر أهم وجه تشابه، واشرح ما يُظهره الطبقان عن الناس، واختم بفكرة أخيرة (دعوة أو أمنية أو تفضيلك مع السبب). والتزم بطول 150–200 كلمة.\n\nانتبه: استخدم المضارع البسيط للحقائق، وmandi comes بالمفرد."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:30","narration":"Kabsa or mandi? Biryani or paella? Today, you become a food reporter!","on_screen":"My Dish Comparison 🍛🥘","visual":"Four steaming dishes appear on a world map."},{"time":"00:30–01:20","narration":"Each fact file has the same features: origin, ingredients, cooking method, flavor and how it is eaten.","on_screen":"Fact Files A–D","visual":"Feature icons line up under each dish."},{"time":"01:20–02:40","narration":"Imagine two huge trays of rice and meat at a family gathering…","on_screen":"(model essay)","visual":"A kabsa pot and a mandi pit oven; family members sharing trays."},{"time":"02:40–03:30","narration":"Introduction: hook + topic sentence. Body: same features, point by point. Conclusion: what the dishes show about people!","on_screen":"P1 · P2 · P3","visual":"Three paragraph blocks build into a menu book."},{"time":"03:30–04:00","narration":"Publish your comparison in our World Food Festival menu book!","on_screen":"World Food Festival 🌍🍽️","visual":"Student pages become a colorful menu."}],"captions_ar":["كبسة أم مندي؟ برياني أم باييلا؟ اليوم تصبح مراسلًا للطعام!","لكل ملف السمات نفسها: الأصل والمكونات وطريقة الطهي والنكهة وطريقة الأكل.","تخيّل صينيتين كبيرتين من الأرز واللحم في تجمع عائلي…","المقدمة: جذب وجملة موضوع. المتن: السمات نفسها نقطة بنقطة. الخاتمة: ما يُظهره الطبقان عن الناس!","انشر مقارنتك في كتيّب مهرجان الطعام العالمي!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm male food-reporter voice, speed 1.0. A student reads the model essay."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Traditional Rice Dishes: Fact Files","body":"**File A – Kabsa** · Origin: Saudi Arabia · Main ingredients: basmati rice, chicken or lamb, tomatoes, onions, dried lime and spices such as cardamom, cinnamon and black pepper · Cooking method: the meat is simmered with spices, then the rice is cooked in the same broth in one pot · Flavor: warm, spicy and slightly sour · How it is eaten: often for family lunches on Fridays and on special days, shared from one big tray\n\n**File B – Mandi** · Origin: the Hadramawt region of Yemen · Main ingredients: rice, lamb or chicken and a few simple spices · Cooking method: the meat hangs above the rice inside a deep oven dug into the ground, so its juices drip onto the rice · Flavor: smoky and tender · How it is eaten: very popular across Saudi Arabia, served on large trays at gatherings and in restaurants\n\n**File C – Biryani** · Origin: South Asia (India and Pakistan) · Main ingredients: basmati rice, spiced meat, yogurt, fried onions and saffron · Cooking method: the rice is partly cooked, then layered with the meat and cooked slowly in a sealed pot · Flavor: rich and fragrant · How it is eaten: a favorite at weddings and celebrations\n\n**File D – Paella** · Origin: Valencia, Spain · Main ingredients: short-grain rice, saffron, chicken and vegetables or seafood · Cooking method: cooked in a wide, shallow pan and not stirred, so a crispy layer forms on the bottom · Flavor: golden, savory and slightly crispy · How it is eaten: often at Sunday family lunches, straight from the pan","word_count":243,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L06-U4-LS5-1","genre":"fact files","role":"model","difficulty":"700L–850L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **two dishes**. Compare them using the **same features**: origin, ingredients, cooking method, flavor and how they are eaten.","Use the **point-by-point** method in your body paragraph (or the block method if you prefer – but don't mix them).","Plan **three paragraphs**: (1) introduction with a hook and a topic sentence naming both dishes and the features; (2) body comparing the features; (3) conclusion with the main similarity, what the dishes show about people and a final thought."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the fact files and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ ملفات الحقائق والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where does mandi come from?","answer":"the Hadramawt region of Yemen","explanation":"File B.","explanation_ar":"حضرموت في اليمن.","options":["Valencia, Spain","the Hadramawt region of Yemen","India and Pakistan","Riyadh"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How is biryani cooked?","answer":"The rice is partly cooked, then layered with meat and cooked slowly in a sealed pot.","explanation":"File C.","explanation_ar":"طبقات تُطهى ببطء.","options":["It is cooked in a wide, shallow pan without stirring.","The rice is partly cooked, then layered with meat and cooked slowly in a sealed pot.","The meat hangs above the rice in an underground oven.","It is grilled on a fire."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why does paella get a crispy layer on the bottom?","answer":"It is not stirred while it cooks in a shallow pan.","explanation":"File D.","explanation_ar":"لأنها لا تُحرّك.","options":["It is fried in oil first.","It is baked in an oven.","Saffron makes it crispy.","It is not stirred while it cooks in a shallow pan."],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which dish is a favorite at weddings in South Asia?","answer":"biryani","explanation":"File C.","explanation_ar":"البرياني.","options":["biryani","paella","mandi","kabsa"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why does mandi have a smoky flavor?","answer":"Its meat cooks above the rice in a deep oven dug into the ground.","explanation":"Cause.","explanation_ar":"بسبب طريقة الطهي.","options":["Smoke is added at the table.","It has dried lime.","It is cooked with saffron.","Its meat cooks above the rice in a deep oven dug into the ground."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which method does the model use in its body paragraph?","answer":"point-by-point","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","options":["block","a recipe list","point-by-point","a timeline"],"dok":2,"skill":"point-by-point","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan your comparison. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط لمقارنتك: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for comparing biryani and paella?","answer":"Biryani and paella are both famous rice dishes, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة موضوع.","options":["I like biryani.","Paella is from Spain.","Biryani and paella are both famous rice dishes, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor.","Rice is good."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence compares the SAME feature for both dishes?","answer":"Biryani comes from South Asia, whereas paella comes from Spain.","explanation":"Same feature.","explanation_ar":"السمة نفسها.","options":["Biryani comes from South Asia, whereas paella comes from Spain.","Biryani comes from South Asia, whereas paella is cooked in a pan.","Biryani has saffron, whereas paella is eaten on Sundays.","Biryani is fragrant, whereas paella comes from Spain."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about mandi is correct?","answer":"Mandi comes from Yemen and is popular in Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Present simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Mandi come from Yemen and is popular in Saudi Arabia.","Mandi coming from Yemen and is popular in Saudi Arabia.","Mandi comes from Yemen and is popular in Saudi Arabia.","Mandi is come from Yemen and is popular in Saudi Arabia."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which SIMILARITY between kabsa and paella is supported by the fact files?","answer":"Both are often shared by families from one big dish.","explanation":"Similarity.","explanation_ar":"كلاهما يُشارك من طبق واحد.","options":["Both come from Spain.","Both are often shared by families from one big dish.","Both use dried lime.","Both are cooked underground."],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which conclusion sentence explains what the dishes show about people?","answer":"Both dishes show that families in many cultures love to share meals together.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"يُظهر قيمة المشاركة.","options":["Biryani has yogurt.","Paella is yellow.","That is my comparison.","Both dishes show that families in many cultures love to share meals together."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which final thought would make the BEST ending for a comparison of kabsa and biryani?","answer":"Next time your family cooks rice, why not try a new recipe from another country?","explanation":"Final thought.","explanation_ar":"أفضل فكرة ختامية.","options":["Kabsa has chicken.","Biryani is from India.","I am finished now.","Next time your family cooks rice, why not try a new recipe from another country?"],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your comparison with the Comparison Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لمقارنتك بمخطط المقارنة.","items":[{"q":"Hook and topic sentence.","answer":"Hook: One dish arrives in a sealed pot, the other in a wide, flat pan. Topic sentence: Biryani and paella are both famous rice dishes, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor.","explanation":"P1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة 1.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Features 1 and 2: origin and cooking method for both dishes.","answer":"Origin: biryani – South Asia; paella – Valencia, Spain. Method: biryani – partly cooked rice layered with meat, cooked slowly in a sealed pot; paella – wide, shallow pan, not stirred.","explanation":"Features.","explanation_ar":"السمات.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Feature 3 and one similarity.","answer":"Flavor: biryani – rich and fragrant; paella – golden, savory and slightly crispy. Similarity: both use saffron and are eaten at family celebrations or gatherings.","explanation":"Feature 3.","explanation_ar":"السمة 3.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Conclusion notes.","answer":"both bring families together; food shows how people celebrate; try both at our World Food Festival","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your comparison.","instructions_ar":"اكتب مقارنتك وحسّنها ودققها وانشرها.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: hook and topic sentence.","answer":"One dish arrives in a sealed pot, the other in a wide, flat pan. Biryani and paella are both famous rice dishes, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: compare three features point by point.","answer":"First, biryani comes from South Asia, whereas paella comes from Valencia in Spain. Second, biryani rice is partly cooked and then layered with spiced meat in a sealed pot, while paella is cooked in a wide, shallow pan and is never stirred. As a result, biryani tastes rich and fragrant, but paella is savory with a crispy bottom. Both dishes, however, use saffron.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثانية.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 3: conclusion.","answer":"In the end, both dishes are served at happy family occasions, which shows that in many cultures food is a way to celebrate together. Come to our World Food Festival and taste the difference!","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الثالثة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence from your biryani-and-paella essay that you improved (before → after).","answer":"Before: Biryani is from India, paella is in a pan. After: Biryani comes from South Asia, whereas paella comes from Spain.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Comparison Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Choose two dishes. Plan the same features for both, then your conclusion.","items":[{"q":"Hook","answer":"Two huge trays at a family gathering – one spicy and golden, one smoky and tender."},{"q":"Topic sentence (both dishes + features)","answer":"Kabsa and mandi: loved in Saudi Arabia; differ in origin, cooking method and flavor."},{"q":"Feature 1: origin","answer":"kabsa – Saudi Arabia / mandi – Hadramawt, Yemen"},{"q":"Feature 2: cooking method","answer":"kabsa – one pot, rice in broth / mandi – meat hangs above rice in an oven dug into the ground"},{"q":"Feature 3: flavor + one similarity","answer":"kabsa – warm, spicy, slightly sour / mandi – smoky, tender; both served on large trays and shared"},{"q":"Conclusion (main similarity, what it shows, final thought)","answer":"both bring people together; Saudis value generosity and sharing; try both and decide!"}]},{"id":"RW-L06-U4-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Choose two dishes from the fact files. Write a three-paragraph comparison (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and a topic sentence that names both dishes and the features you will compare. Paragraph 2: compare at least three features with facts and comparison words, using one method. Paragraph 3: the main similarity, what the dishes show about the people who eat them and a final thought. Choose two dishes from the fact files. Write a three-paragraph comparison (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and a topic sentence that names both dishes and the features you will compare. Paragraph 2: compare at least three features with facts and comparison words, using one method. Paragraph 3: the main similarity, what the dishes show about the people who eat them and a final thought.","instructions_ar":"اختر طبقين من ملفات الحقائق، واكتب مقارنة من ثلاث فقرات (150–200 كلمة). الفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب وجملة موضوع تذكر الطبقين والسمات. الثانية: قارن ثلاث سمات على الأقل بالحقائق وكلمات المقارنة بطريقة واحدة. الثالثة: أهم وجه تشابه وما يُظهره الطبقان عن الناس وفكرة أخيرة.","prompt":"Choose two dishes from the fact files. Write a three-paragraph comparison (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and a topic sentence that names both dishes and the features you will compare. Paragraph 2: compare at least three features with facts and comparison words, using one method. Paragraph 3: the main similarity, what the dishes show about the people who eat them and a final thought.","checklist":["My hook makes the reader hungry to read on.","My topic sentence names both dishes and the features.","I compared the same features for both dishes.","I used one method (point-by-point or block) clearly.","I used at least four comparison words.","My facts come from the fact files, in my own words.","My conclusion gives the main similarity, what it shows about people and a final thought.","My essay has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":150,"max_words":220,"organizer":"Comparison Planner (hook – topic sentence – three features – conclusion)","items":[{"q":"Choose two dishes from the fact files. Write a three-paragraph comparison (150–200 words). Paragraph 1: a hook and a topic sentence that names both dishes and the features you will compare. Paragraph 2: compare at least three features with facts and comparison words, using one method. Paragraph 3: the main similarity, what the dishes show about the people who eat them and a final thought.","answer":"Imagine two huge trays of rice and meat at a family gathering – one spicy and golden, the other smoky and tender. Kabsa and mandi are two of the most loved rice dishes in Saudi Arabia, but they differ in their origin, cooking method and flavor. (new paragraph) First, kabsa comes from Saudi Arabia, whereas mandi comes from the Hadramawt region of Yemen. Second, kabsa is cooked in one pot: the meat is simmered with spices, and then the rice is cooked in the same broth. Mandi, on the other hand, is cooked in a deep oven dug into the ground, where the meat hangs above the rice and its juices drip down. As a result, kabsa tastes warm, spicy and slightly sour, while mandi has a smoky flavor. Both dishes, however, are served on large trays and shared with family and guests. (new paragraph) In the end, both dishes are more than food: they bring people together and show how much Saudis value generosity. If you ever visit, try both – and decide which one you like best!","_w":{"checklist":["My hook makes the reader hungry to read on.","My topic sentence names both dishes and the features.","I compared the same features for both dishes.","I used one method (point-by-point or block) clearly.","I used at least four comparison words.","My facts come from the fact files, in my own words.","My conclusion gives the main similarity, what it shows about people and a final thought.","My essay has three paragraphs and 150–200 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":150,"max":220}}]}],"game":{"name":"World Food Festival","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Festival stalls: the child visits four virtual food stalls, answers a comparison question at each (e.g., 'How is mandi different from kabsa?') and collects stamps in a food passport.","Half the class runs 'stalls' with their comparisons; the other half are visitors.","Visitors ask each stall one comparison question from their passport.","Stall holders must answer with a comparison word (both, whereas, unlike…).","Visitors stamp the passport for a correct, complete answer.","Switch roles halfway; the most stamps wins a 'Master Chef of Comparison' badge."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"How many paragraphs does your dish comparison have?","answer":"three","explanation":"Organization.","explanation_ar":"ثلاث.","options":["one","two","three","five"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"What does *fragrant* mean?","answer":"having a sweet or pleasant smell","explanation":"Vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"عطِر.","options":["very spicy","having a sweet or pleasant smell","very cold","very old"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The mandi meat is very **tender**.* *Tender* means ___.","answer":"soft and easy to chew","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"طري.","options":["very hard","very salty","burned","soft and easy to chew"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What should the topic sentence of your comparison include?","answer":"both dishes and the features you will compare","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"الطبقان والسمات.","options":["only your favorite dish","both dishes and the features you will compare","a recipe","the price of each dish"],"dok":1,"skill":"topic sentences"},{"q":"Which sentence about paella is correct?","answer":"Paella is cooked in a wide, shallow pan.","explanation":"Passive.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Paella cook in a wide, shallow pan.","Paella is cook in a wide, shallow pan.","Paella cooking in a wide, shallow pan.","Paella is cooked in a wide, shallow pan."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which comparison word introduces a DIFFERENCE?","answer":"whereas","explanation":"Comparison word.","explanation_ar":"whereas.","options":["whereas","both","similarly","also"],"dok":2,"skill":"comparison words"},{"q":"Why must you compare the same features for both dishes?","answer":"so the reader can clearly see how the dishes are alike and different","explanation":"Reasoning.","explanation_ar":"لتتضح المقارنة.","options":["so the essay is shorter","so you don't need facts","so the reader can clearly see how the dishes are alike and different","so both dishes sound the same"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare and contrast"},{"q":"Which detail from the fact files supports the claim that kabsa is a shared family meal?","answer":"It is shared from one big tray on Fridays and special days.","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"يُشارك من صينية واحدة.","options":["It is shared from one big tray on Fridays and special days.","It uses basmati rice.","It has dried lime.","It comes from Saudi Arabia."],"dok":2,"skill":"supporting evidence"},{"q":"A partner's conclusion just repeats the topic sentence. What is the BEST advice?","answer":"Add the main similarity, what the dishes show about people and a final thought.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف التشابه والمعنى والفكرة الأخيرة.","options":["Add the main similarity, what the dishes show about people and a final thought.","Delete the conclusion.","Add more cooking steps.","Change the dishes."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"What can comparing traditional dishes teach us about other cultures?","answer":"how people live, celebrate and what they value","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"كيف يعيش الناس وما يقدّرونه.","options":["only how much food costs","how people live, celebrate and what they value","nothing important","which country is best"],"dok":3,"skill":"cultural awareness"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'region': a map with one area highlighted"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tender': a fork pulling soft meat apart"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'smoky': smoke rising from an underground oven"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fragrant': steam rising from a pot of rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'savory': a pan of golden rice"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gathering': many people around a large tray"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'menu': a colorful restaurant menu"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-01","prompt":"A student-made World Food Festival menu book."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-02","prompt":"Four traditional rice dish fact files."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L06-U4-LS5-03","prompt":"A tray-shaped comparison planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V01","script":"region. Mandi comes from the Hadramawt region of Yemen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V02","script":"tender. The mandi meat is very tender."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V03","script":"smoky. Mandi has a smoky flavor."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V04","script":"fragrant. Biryani is rich and fragrant."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V05","script":"savory. Paella is a savory dish."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V06","script":"gathering. Mandi is served at big gatherings."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-V07","script":"menu. Our class made a World Food Festival menu."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-T1","script":"**File A – Kabsa** · Origin: Saudi Arabia · Main ingredients: basmati rice, chicken or lamb, tomatoes, onions, dried lime and spices such as cardamom, cinnamon and black pepper · Cooking method: the meat is simmered with spices, then the rice is cooked in the same broth in one pot · Flavor: warm, spicy and slightly sour · How it is eaten: often for family lunches on Fridays and on special days, shared from one big tray **File B – Mandi** · Origin: the Hadramawt region of Yemen · Main ingredients: rice, lamb or chicken and a few simple spices · Cooking method: the meat hangs above the rice inside a deep oven dug into the ground, so its juices drip onto the rice · Flavor: smoky and tender · How it is eaten: very popular across Saudi Arabia, served on large trays at gatherings and in restaurants **File C – Biryani** · Origin: South Asia (India and Pakistan) · Main ingredients: basmati rice, spiced meat, yogurt, fried onions and saffron · Cooking method: the rice is partly cooked, then layered with the meat and cooked slowly in a sealed pot · Flavor: rich and fragrant · How it is eaten: a favorite at weddings and celebrations **File D – Paella** · Origin: Valencia, Spain · Main ingredients: short-grain rice, saffron, chicken and vegetables or seafood · Cooking method: cooked in a wide, shallow pan and not stirred, so a crispy layer forms on the bottom · Flavor: golden, savory and slightly crispy · How it is eaten: often at Sunday family lunches, straight from the pan"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L06-U4-LS5-VID","script":"Kabsa or mandi? Biryani or paella? Today, you become a food reporter! Each fact file has the same features: origin, ingredients, cooking method, flavor and how it is eaten. Imagine two huge trays of rice and meat at a family gathering… Introduction: hook + topic sentence. Body: same features, point by point. Conclusion: what the dishes show about people! Publish your comparison in our World Food Festival menu book!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Saffron is an expensive ___ that turns rice golden.","answer":"spice","explanation":"Spice.","explanation_ar":"بهار.","options":["spice","crop","portion","tray"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Read the ___ carefully before you start cooking.","answer":"recipe","explanation":"Recipe.","explanation_ar":"الوصفة.","options":["harvest","recipe","grain","custom"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Farmers in Al-Qassim ___ their dates at the end of summer.","answer":"harvest","explanation":"Harvest.","explanation_ar":"يحصدون.","options":["bake","simmer","harvest","sprinkle"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Moroccan ___ is famous for couscous and tagine.","answer":"cuisine","explanation":"Cuisine.","explanation_ar":"المطبخ.","options":["cuisine","portion","broth","flavor"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"___ the onions into small pieces before you fry them.","answer":"Chop","explanation":"chop onions.","explanation_ar":"قطّع.","options":["Boil","Chop","Bake","Grill"],"dok":1,"skill":"collocations","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Add a ___ of parsley to the salad.","answer":"bunch","explanation":"a bunch of parsley.","explanation_ar":"حزمة.","options":["pinch","slice","loaf","bunch"],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence uses a natural food collocation?","answer":"This soup has a strong flavor of cumin.","explanation":"strong flavor.","explanation_ar":"strong flavor.","options":["This soup has a big flavor of cumin.","This soup has a tall flavor of cumin.","This soup has a strong flavor of cumin.","This soup has a heavy flavor of cumin."],"dok":2,"skill":"collocations","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The two rice dishes **differ** mainly in how they are cooked.* *Differ* means ___.","answer":"are not the same","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يختلفان.","options":["are very old","are cooked together","are expensive","are not the same"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which word in this sentence shows a difference? *Kabsa is cooked in a pot, whereas mandi is cooked underground.*","answer":"whereas","explanation":"Comparison word.","explanation_ar":"whereas.","options":["cooked","pot","whereas","is"],"dok":1,"skill":"comparison words","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about dates is correct?","answer":"Dates grow on palm trees.","explanation":"Present simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Dates grows on palm trees.","Dates grow on palm trees.","Dates growing on palm trees.","Dates is grow on palm trees."],"dok":1,"skill":"grammar","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence compares onigiri and mutabbaq POINT BY POINT?","answer":"Onigiri is served cold, while mutabbaq is served hot.","explanation":"Point-by-point.","explanation_ar":"النقطة بنقطة.","options":["Onigiri is a Japanese rice ball.","Mutabbaq is a Saudi street food.","Onigiri is served cold, while mutabbaq is served hot.","I tried mutabbaq last week."],"dok":2,"skill":"point-by-point","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST topic sentence for comparing Saudi coffee and Turkish coffee?","answer":"Saudi coffee and Turkish coffee are both served to guests, but they differ in color, flavor and the way they are prepared.","explanation":"Topic sentence.","explanation_ar":"أفضل جملة موضوع.","options":["Saudi coffee and Turkish coffee are both served to guests, but they differ in color, flavor and the way they are prepared.","Coffee is a drink.","I don't like coffee.","Turkish coffee is from Turkey."],"dok":2,"skill":"topic sentences","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"In a BLOCK comparison of tea and coffee, which order is correct?","answer":"tea: origin, taste, how it is served → coffee: origin, taste, how it is served","explanation":"Block method.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الصحيح.","options":["tea: origin → coffee: taste → tea: price → coffee: color","coffee: origin → tea: color → coffee: price","taste of both → origin of tea only","tea: origin, taste, how it is served → coffee: origin, taste, how it is served"],"dok":2,"skill":"block method","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which conclusion is STRONGEST for an essay comparing kabsa and onigiri?","answer":"Although one is shared on a big tray and the other fits in a lunchbox, both show how rice brings people together.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"أقوى خاتمة.","options":["Kabsa is hot.","Although one is shared on a big tray and the other fits in a lunchbox, both show how rice brings people together.","That is my comparison.","Onigiri has seaweed."],"dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a three-paragraph comparison (150–200 words) of a traditional Saudi breakfast and a breakfast from another country that you know. Paragraph 1: a hook and a topic sentence naming both breakfasts and the features you will compare. Paragraph 2: compare at least three features with comparison words, using one method. Paragraph 3: the main similarity, what the breakfasts show about the people and a final thought.","model":"What does the first meal of the day look like in Riyadh and in Tokyo? A traditional Saudi breakfast and a traditional Japanese breakfast are both warm, filling meals, but they differ in their main foods, flavors and drinks. (new paragraph) First, a Saudi breakfast often includes ful, which is fava beans cooked with oil, cumin and lemon, served with fresh tamees bread. A Japanese breakfast, on the other hand, usually has a bowl of steamed rice, grilled fish and miso soup. Second, the Saudi meal tastes rich and slightly spicy, whereas the Japanese meal tastes light and salty. Finally, Saudis often drink sweet tea with mint, while Japanese families usually drink green tea without sugar. However, both breakfasts are eaten slowly with the family, especially on weekends. (new paragraph) In the end, both meals show that in each culture, breakfast is a time to share food and start the day together. Perhaps one day you can try both and decide which one gives you more energy!","marks":20}});
